WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.166 --> 00:00:02.602
Some scenes in this program have been reenacted,

00:00:02.902 --> 00:00:06.006
including the depiction of certain crime scene investigations.

00:00:12.312 --> 00:00:16.216
A man retrieves the burned remains of someone who may be his brother.

00:00:18.218 --> 00:00:20.120
But how can he really be sure?

00:00:22.956 --> 00:00:25.058
Under the rubble of a burned-down house,

00:00:25.425 --> 00:00:27.894
investigators find the shattered remnants of a body.

00:00:30.864 --> 00:00:32.699
Can they piece together what happened?

00:00:35.301 --> 00:00:41.541
A scientist must identify a woman from scraps of burned bones weighing less than three paper clips.

00:00:43.877 --> 00:00:45.412
Will he be up to the challenge?

00:00:48.314 --> 00:00:51.351
The fate of a murder investigation hangs in the balance.

00:00:53.319 --> 00:00:53.987
For centuries,

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:57.657
killers have relied on fire to conceal their crimes.

00:01:35.462 --> 00:01:35.795
Fire.

00:01:37.363 --> 00:01:40.700
Medieval alchemists saw it as a tool of transformation.

00:01:41.768 --> 00:01:42.535
Used correctly,

00:01:42.969 --> 00:01:46.272
it could turn dull lead into gleaming gold.

00:01:47.941 --> 00:01:49.542
Fire does transform,

00:01:50.076 --> 00:01:51.978
but not in the way the alchemists hoped.

00:01:54.047 --> 00:01:55.982
It is a process called oxidation,

00:01:56.683 --> 00:02:00.720
the speedy marriage of convenience between oxygen and another substance.

00:02:01.921 --> 00:02:09.996
The union creates new compounds like carbon and water while releasing enormous amounts of energy in the form of heat and light.

00:02:13.366 --> 00:02:15.135
All fires need an initiator,

00:02:16.236 --> 00:02:17.837
a spark to get them going.

00:02:19.806 --> 00:02:23.176
It's a little boost of energy that sets off the chain reaction.

00:02:28.848 --> 00:02:32.085
And the fire burns until there's nothing left to burn.

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:34.787
In Ohio,

00:02:34.787 --> 00:02:36.422
in 1994,

00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:38.558
Danny King started a fire,

00:02:39.993 --> 00:02:43.963
initiating a chain of events that transformed his entire world.

00:02:46.666 --> 00:02:49.802
King had a fiery relationship with his live-in girlfriend,

00:02:50.069 --> 00:02:51.070
Marilyn Garland.

00:02:51.104 --> 00:02:51.237
England.

00:02:52.105 --> 00:02:56.009
They had been together on and off for years and had a three-year-old child.

00:02:59.012 --> 00:03:00.180
But one March night,

00:03:00.547 --> 00:03:02.615
things spun horribly out of control.

00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:06.986
Danny King shot and killed Marilyn Garland.

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:14.460
Then he tried to hide every trace of his crime.

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:22.001
He set the body afire and kept it burning in a metal drum for two days.

00:03:24.237 --> 00:03:24.837
Occasionally,

00:03:25.138 --> 00:03:27.407
he'd stir the brittle bones with a stick,

00:03:28.208 --> 00:03:30.276
burning them until they would burn no more.

00:03:33.746 --> 00:03:37.350
He took what was left and smashed it into little pieces with a hammer.

00:03:43.289 --> 00:03:45.792
He gathered the remains and dumped them in the river.

00:03:47.961 --> 00:03:49.562
He may have thought that without a body,

00:03:49.996 --> 00:03:51.698
there could be no proof of murder.

00:03:56.269 --> 00:03:59.305
Then he ditched the drum behind a warehouse in town,

00:03:59.706 --> 00:04:01.541
a place where similar drums stood.

00:04:02.742 --> 00:04:04.677
Certain he had gotten away with his crime,

00:04:05.144 --> 00:04:10.483
Danny King waited six days before he called the police to report Garland as a missing person.

00:04:12.952 --> 00:04:15.154
Lieutenant Ray Todd worked on the case.

00:04:16.122 --> 00:04:19.792
Danny King reported Marilyn missing on the 26th,

00:04:20.293 --> 00:04:20.526
okay,

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:22.228
and said that she'd been gone for a week.

00:04:22.228 --> 00:04:22.962
Well,

00:04:22.996 --> 00:04:28.268
it wasn't unusual for Marilyn to just take off for a few days at a time and go party.

00:04:30.036 --> 00:04:30.703
In his office,

00:04:31.004 --> 00:04:33.940
Lieutenant Todd reviewed King's missing persons report.

00:04:35.208 --> 00:04:36.709
Despite Garland's reputation,

00:04:37.143 --> 00:04:38.478
he had to take it seriously.

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:43.583
He was joined on the case by Detective Gregory Macko.

00:04:44.684 --> 00:04:48.955
Macko found that Garland's family was taking the disappearance very seriously.

00:04:50.189 --> 00:04:51.891
We talked to other family members,

00:04:52.425 --> 00:04:53.893
getting conflicting stories,

00:04:53.926 --> 00:04:55.495
some saying this is not her.

00:04:56.162 --> 00:04:58.931
This was her years ago when she was wild and free,

00:04:58.965 --> 00:05:00.333
but now she had a little boy.

00:05:00.733 --> 00:05:01.701
She wouldn't do this.

00:05:02.101 --> 00:05:03.736
She wouldn't leave the little boy behind.

00:05:03.836 --> 00:05:04.704
Something's wrong.

00:05:05.104 --> 00:05:08.007
They didn't believe that she just up and packed up and left.

00:05:08.341 --> 00:05:10.476
They actually believed that something had happened to her.

00:05:12.545 --> 00:05:15.148
Detective Macko began investigating more thoroughly.

00:05:17.183 --> 00:05:20.520
It was standard procedure to interview the person who filed the report.

00:05:21.421 --> 00:05:23.790
So they called in Danny King for questioning.

00:05:24.724 --> 00:05:25.391
He's a boyfriend,

00:05:25.391 --> 00:05:26.459
he's going to be a suspect,

00:05:27.327 --> 00:05:29.762
and our thinking was let's eliminate him immediately.

00:05:31.030 --> 00:05:32.365
That wasn't how it worked out.

00:05:33.166 --> 00:05:35.134
The interview took an unexpected turn.

00:05:37.203 --> 00:05:38.638
After that interview process,

00:05:38.738 --> 00:05:42.008
we asked him if he would be willing to take a polygraph,

00:05:42.008 --> 00:05:42.875
a lie detector test,

00:05:43.876 --> 00:05:45.845
and he kind of hedged on it at first.

00:05:46.612 --> 00:05:48.548
So it just threw red flags.

00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:53.753
They arranged the test for the next day and dismissed King.

00:05:56.356 --> 00:05:57.623
King should have gone home,

00:05:58.157 --> 00:05:58.758
but he didn't.

00:06:01.461 --> 00:06:02.362
He went to a bar,

00:06:02.829 --> 00:06:04.163
concerned about the polygraph,

00:06:04.430 --> 00:06:05.298
and began drinking.

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:10.903
He met a drinking buddy there and confided that he was worried about the test.

00:06:13.439 --> 00:06:14.173
The more he drank,

00:06:14.440 --> 00:06:15.274
the more he talked.

00:06:16.142 --> 00:06:18.244
Soon he confessed to killing Garland.

00:06:20.313 --> 00:06:22.515
His friend could tell King was serious.

00:06:23.182 --> 00:06:25.918
He felt he had no choice but to go to the police.

00:06:28.154 --> 00:06:36.696
He proceeded to tell me that he just left the bar where him and Danny King were drinking for a couple of hours and that Danny King had confessed to him.

00:06:37.397 --> 00:06:40.032
Now the police had three facts to go on.

00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:41.401
A missing woman,

00:06:42.101 --> 00:06:44.070
a second-hand barroom confession,

00:06:44.737 --> 00:06:47.673
and a suspect who was nervous about a polygraph test.

00:06:49.909 --> 00:06:50.877
In terms of evidence,

00:06:51.110 --> 00:06:52.612
it was shaky at best.

00:06:53.513 --> 00:06:59.352
But Detective Macko and Lieutenant Todd felt it was enough to arrest King on suspicion of murder.

00:07:01.721 --> 00:07:04.557
King lived with a small child and a large dog,

00:07:05.024 --> 00:07:07.927
so the police felt uneasy about arresting him at his house.

00:07:09.395 --> 00:07:12.532
They decided to wait it out and apprehend him the next morning.

00:07:16.402 --> 00:07:18.871
They had no idea how violent he might be,

00:07:19.472 --> 00:07:20.973
but if he did commit this crime,

00:07:21.307 --> 00:07:23.609
they knew they were dealing with a dangerous man.

00:07:26.579 --> 00:07:28.781
Police called in the SWAT team for backup.

00:07:34.854 --> 00:07:35.955
As King went off to work,

00:07:36.289 --> 00:07:39.525
they forced his truck into a roadblock and made their move.

00:07:45.198 --> 00:07:49.802
Put your hands up Put them up Put your hands up Where are you going?

00:07:49.802 --> 00:07:50.069
Get out of the van.

00:07:50.069 --> 00:07:50.570
You're under arrest,

00:07:50.570 --> 00:07:50.670
right?

00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:51.037
Put your hands up.

00:07:51.637 --> 00:07:53.473
After King was safely in custody,

00:07:53.806 --> 00:07:55.775
the police began to build their case.

00:07:56.742 --> 00:07:57.143
For that,

00:07:57.443 --> 00:07:58.945
they needed more evidence.

00:08:00.346 --> 00:08:03.282
They obtained a search warrant and inspected King's house,

00:08:03.749 --> 00:08:05.351
but they found nothing incriminating.

00:08:06.652 --> 00:08:10.056
They had divers search the river and the canal for remains.

00:08:10.857 --> 00:08:11.991
They came up dry.

00:08:14.827 --> 00:08:15.561
Without a body,

00:08:15.928 --> 00:08:16.395
a weapon,

00:08:16.729 --> 00:08:18.297
or any other evidence against him,

00:08:18.764 --> 00:08:20.533
Lieutenant Todd didn't have a case,

00:08:21.033 --> 00:08:23.002
and Danny King would go free.

00:08:25.037 --> 00:08:28.641
If King truly did try to destroy the evidence in a metal drum,

00:08:28.774 --> 00:08:29.375
they reasoned,

00:08:29.642 --> 00:08:31.644
the drum must be around somewhere.

00:08:32.678 --> 00:08:35.147
But all they had was a burned patch of grass.

00:08:36.883 --> 00:08:39.785
The police placed an ad in the paper to find the drum.

00:08:43.523 --> 00:08:44.824
Soon after the ad ran,

00:08:45.124 --> 00:08:47.527
police received a call from a local businessman.

00:08:48.461 --> 00:08:52.832
One of his employees noticed a suspicious-looking barrel behind their warehouse.

00:08:54.934 --> 00:08:56.969
Lieutenant Todd went out to inspect it.

00:08:59.939 --> 00:09:02.408
You could see some debris inside the bottom.

00:09:03.209 --> 00:09:08.180
Reached in and picked up what I believed to be was a bone fragment.

00:09:08.781 --> 00:09:09.549
When I saw that,

00:09:09.615 --> 00:09:14.353
I just knew that this was the barrel that was used to burn the body of Merrill Garland.

00:09:16.455 --> 00:09:20.526
Todd shook the drum to remove more debris clinging to its interior.

00:09:21.594 --> 00:09:22.562
Out came nails,

00:09:22.895 --> 00:09:23.763
scraps of wire,

00:09:24.063 --> 00:09:24.630
some fabric,

00:09:24.964 --> 00:09:25.865
and a seed pod.

00:09:27.700 --> 00:09:29.535
There's one seed in this vial here,

00:09:30.303 --> 00:09:31.737
and this was found in the barrel.

00:09:33.039 --> 00:09:35.241
We didn't know where it may have come from.

00:09:36.075 --> 00:09:36.375
Later,

00:09:36.409 --> 00:09:39.111
we had it identified as a catawba seed.

00:09:39.679 --> 00:09:41.447
It's kind of an unusual tree,

00:09:41.447 --> 00:09:43.049
and there's not that many in the area.

00:09:44.116 --> 00:09:48.688
And there is a catawba tree at 125 Canal Street,

00:09:49.422 --> 00:09:54.560
which is where the murder took place and where Danny burned Marilyn's body in the barrel.

00:09:56.062 --> 00:09:56.829
But potentially,

00:09:57.129 --> 00:10:00.933
the most damning evidence pulled from the drum was also the smallest,

00:10:01.667 --> 00:10:03.269
tiny shards of bone.

00:10:05.504 --> 00:10:07.506
If Todd could prove they were the victims,

00:10:07.907 --> 00:10:08.841
he'd have a case.

00:10:09.842 --> 00:10:11.911
He sent them to the coroner for analysis.

00:10:13.746 --> 00:10:16.449
The coroner was fairly sure the bones were human.

00:10:17.950 --> 00:10:18.317
However,

00:10:18.317 --> 00:10:23.856
he wanted them looked at by a top forensic anthropologist.

00:10:25.992 --> 00:10:30.296
He contacted Doug Owsley of the Smithsonian Institution in Washington,

00:10:30.329 --> 00:10:30.596
D.C.

00:10:31.897 --> 00:10:36.502
Owsley has built an international reputation by identifying human remains.

00:10:38.204 --> 00:10:42.241
This wasn't the first time Owsley had worked with the Summit County Coroner's Office.

00:10:42.975 --> 00:10:44.010
A few years earlier,

00:10:44.276 --> 00:10:47.947
he helped them analyze the remains of Jeffrey Dahmer's first victim.

00:10:49.115 --> 00:10:51.450
We've had cases where they involve dismemberment,

00:10:51.484 --> 00:10:54.754
where they will dismember parts of the body and they'll put them in different locations.

00:10:55.187 --> 00:11:01.327
We've had cases where they've used acids and corrosives and disfigurement of the body to try and make it difficult to say who that person is.

00:11:01.894 --> 00:11:05.164
They all tell you something about the individual behind that process,

00:11:05.598 --> 00:11:06.399
and in the same sense,

00:11:06.399 --> 00:11:08.167
they all leave different kinds of evidence.

00:11:10.136 --> 00:11:11.771
In the case of Marilyn Garland,

00:11:12.238 --> 00:11:15.241
Owsley was literally scraping the bottom of the barrel.

00:11:16.409 --> 00:11:21.180
The evidence he had to work with consisted of 2.9 grams of bone fragments,

00:11:21.881 --> 00:11:23.883
about the weight of three paper clips.

00:11:25.518 --> 00:11:28.421
He also had three tiny shards of tooth enamel.

00:11:29.689 --> 00:11:34.527
All had been on fire for two days and were shrunken and distorted by the process.

00:11:35.561 --> 00:11:38.597
Could Owsley make sense of the paltry slivers?

00:11:39.765 --> 00:11:44.136
Looks like.

00:11:44.136 --> 00:11:49.442
Forensic scientist Doug Owsley faced many challenges in his work on the Marilyn Garland murder.

00:11:50.376 --> 00:11:54.613
The first was confirming whether the bone fragments from the metal drum were human.

00:11:56.582 --> 00:12:01.353
In this instance we got really lucky because one of the fragments that was preserved was this finger bone.

00:12:01.954 --> 00:12:02.822
And if you think about it,

00:12:02.822 --> 00:12:09.295
different animals out there have very distinctive foot and hand bone structure.

00:12:09.795 --> 00:12:11.263
Think of a hoof for instance.

00:12:11.263 --> 00:12:11.530
instance,

00:12:12.164 --> 00:12:15.434
or what the foot of a dog looks like.

00:12:15.434 --> 00:12:17.670
It's very different than what we see in the human.

00:12:18.337 --> 00:12:19.939
And we've got not a complete bone,

00:12:19.939 --> 00:12:21.373
but actually just half of it,

00:12:21.407 --> 00:12:26.045
but it's enough of the bone for us to actually be able to look at that and say.

00:12:26.512 --> 00:12:26.645
Well,

00:12:26.645 --> 00:12:27.613
that's human morphology.

00:12:28.681 --> 00:12:30.750
Owsley's next task was more daunting.

00:12:31.650 --> 00:12:34.553
Could he tell if these were the remains of Marilyn Garland?

00:12:35.755 --> 00:12:36.388
With burning,

00:12:37.056 --> 00:12:39.058
it takes maybe more time to analyze it,

00:12:39.091 --> 00:12:41.093
and you really have to know exactly what you're looking for.

00:12:41.160 --> 00:12:41.761
But you still,

00:12:42.161 --> 00:12:43.462
with the fragments of the bone,

00:12:43.996 --> 00:12:45.364
male skeletons tend to be bigger,

00:12:45.898 --> 00:12:46.499
females tend,

00:12:46.699 --> 00:12:47.166
on average,

00:12:47.166 --> 00:12:48.000
to be smaller.

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:54.807
And so how it is burned and the size that is the result of that process is still going to tell you often something about sex.

00:12:54.907 --> 00:12:56.041
will still tell you something about.

00:12:56.041 --> 00:12:56.509
about age.

00:12:56.942 --> 00:12:59.912
And so it may make the identification much more difficult,

00:13:00.246 --> 00:13:01.147
more time consuming,

00:13:01.313 --> 00:13:02.114
maybe more tedious,

00:13:02.515 --> 00:13:03.849
but there's going to be a lot of evidence there.

00:13:05.351 --> 00:13:07.520
At the outset of the identification process,

00:13:08.020 --> 00:13:10.856
Owsley knew nothing about the victim's physical features.

00:13:12.224 --> 00:13:14.460
That's the way he prefers to work on a case.

00:13:16.028 --> 00:13:20.566
The ideal circumstance often is not to know a great deal about it.

00:13:20.766 --> 00:13:23.002
It's better to be a little bit in the dark on that sort of thing,

00:13:23.002 --> 00:13:24.136
or a lot in the dark about that.

00:13:24.637 --> 00:13:27.239
Let the bones talk to you in terms of what they have to say,

00:13:27.773 --> 00:13:29.275
say what the evidence is from those.

00:13:30.142 --> 00:13:31.043
From the remains,

00:13:31.410 --> 00:13:33.512
Owsley identified two rib fragments.

00:13:33.946 --> 00:13:35.848
The largest was a half inch long.

00:13:36.949 --> 00:13:39.518
He could tell one came from near the breastbone,

00:13:39.919 --> 00:13:41.453
perhaps the fourth or fifth rib.

00:13:42.254 --> 00:13:46.158
It provided the key to unlocking secrets about the body it came from.

00:13:47.760 --> 00:13:48.594
As we age,

00:13:48.894 --> 00:13:52.097
the rounded ends of our bones become more cup-shaped.

00:13:52.865 --> 00:13:58.037
The rib's cupped end suggested the person was between 24 and 40 years old.

00:13:59.638 --> 00:14:03.943
It was 3.4 millimeters thick by 13 millimeters wide.

00:14:04.944 --> 00:14:06.478
Owsley considered that small,

00:14:07.046 --> 00:14:10.649
even after assuming it shrunk up to 25 percent in the fire.

00:14:11.584 --> 00:14:15.921
You can never on a piece of bone that size bet everything.

00:14:16.755 --> 00:14:17.623
But on the other hand,

00:14:17.656 --> 00:14:19.625
you can turn around and say in terms of probability,

00:14:19.692 --> 00:14:21.260
in terms of comparative data that we have,

00:14:22.161 --> 00:14:24.029
it's likely that this is a very small person.

00:14:25.030 --> 00:14:29.001
The size suggested the victim was either a female or a small male.

00:14:30.169 --> 00:14:31.036
To determine which,

00:14:31.370 --> 00:14:36.008
he compared the bone's height and thickness against a standard chart of rib measurements.

00:14:36.976 --> 00:14:37.776
In females,

00:14:37.977 --> 00:14:42.381
the fifth rib is going to have an average width of 4.65 millimeters.

00:14:43.282 --> 00:14:43.782
Males,

00:14:44.183 --> 00:14:46.385
fifth rib is going to be 6.78,

00:14:46.452 --> 00:14:48.554
so it's much larger in terms of its thickness.

00:14:49.421 --> 00:14:52.358
So you work it through as best you can and try and characterize.

00:14:53.192 --> 00:14:54.426
for the entire rib cage,

00:14:54.894 --> 00:14:56.862
male and female values and what the difference is.

00:14:58.230 --> 00:14:58.998
With this bone,

00:14:59.265 --> 00:15:00.466
the answer was clear.

00:15:01.200 --> 00:15:02.334
By all standards,

00:15:02.568 --> 00:15:04.503
the person it came from was petite,

00:15:05.504 --> 00:15:06.939
very likely a female.

00:15:08.607 --> 00:15:10.609
The bones had told him all they could.

00:15:11.377 --> 00:15:13.545
It was time to take a look at the tooth fragments.

00:15:15.114 --> 00:15:17.116
Owsley had only three to work with.

00:15:17.783 --> 00:15:19.852
There were little more than chips of enamel.

00:15:22.988 --> 00:15:24.857
By examining the thickness of the enamel,

00:15:25.224 --> 00:15:27.259
Owsley could tell they were from adult teeth.

00:15:29.261 --> 00:15:32.665
Their shape suggested their approximate position in the victim's mouth.

00:15:34.700 --> 00:15:37.603
They had tell-tale grooves that suggested dental work.

00:15:38.671 --> 00:15:39.638
But it wasn't enough.

00:15:41.373 --> 00:15:45.110
He needed some high-tech help to expose traces of fillings.

00:15:45.978 --> 00:15:48.881
Then he could match them to Garland's dental records.

00:15:51.617 --> 00:15:55.888
Owsley sent the teeth to the Smithsonian's Conservation Analytical Laboratory.

00:15:57.356 --> 00:15:57.656
There,

00:15:57.723 --> 00:16:01.160
they underwent a process called Energy Dispersive Spectroscopy,

00:16:01.827 --> 00:16:02.828
or EDS.

00:16:04.363 --> 00:16:07.066
Most dental fillings are a combination of silver,

00:16:07.433 --> 00:16:07.666
tin,

00:16:08.133 --> 00:16:08.567
mercury,

00:16:08.734 --> 00:16:09.301
and copper.

00:16:10.202 --> 00:16:11.904
They melt in the heat of a fire,

00:16:12.404 --> 00:16:14.406
leaving behind invisible traces.

00:16:15.874 --> 00:16:17.810
The EDS brings them out.

00:16:20.346 --> 00:16:22.348
The tooth fragment is put into the chamber.

00:16:24.049 --> 00:16:25.451
Then the air is drawn out,

00:16:25.751 --> 00:16:26.685
creating a vacuum.

00:16:28.354 --> 00:16:30.723
An electron beam bombards the sample.

00:16:31.490 --> 00:16:33.292
It can be aimed very precisely.

00:16:34.660 --> 00:16:36.929
The EDS provides a 3D image,

00:16:37.196 --> 00:16:39.465
so the operator knows where to point the beam.

00:16:41.734 --> 00:16:44.636
The stream of energy excites electrons in the sample,

00:16:45.037 --> 00:16:46.705
causing some of them to fly off.

00:16:49.074 --> 00:16:51.143
Some elements give off more electrons,

00:16:51.710 --> 00:16:52.778
some produce fewer.

00:16:53.712 --> 00:16:56.382
By measuring the electrons spewing from the sample,

00:16:56.849 --> 00:16:59.685
the EDS determines what elements it's made from.

00:17:01.253 --> 00:17:04.456
The results come out as a graph of peaks and valleys.

00:17:05.257 --> 00:17:07.426
Each peak represents a different element.

00:17:08.427 --> 00:17:11.797
Its height corresponds to the amount of the element in the sample.

00:17:14.099 --> 00:17:17.636
The graph was printed out and given to Owsley to analyze.

00:17:18.837 --> 00:17:20.272
This is an area here on this count,

00:17:20.339 --> 00:17:21.006
fragment one,

00:17:21.573 --> 00:17:22.875
where we're scanning on the tooth surface.

00:17:22.875 --> 00:17:24.276
You can pick up the peak for calcium,

00:17:24.576 --> 00:17:25.444
peak for phosphorus,

00:17:26.145 --> 00:17:28.013
and those are normal tooth enamel.

00:17:28.747 --> 00:17:29.681
On that same fragment,

00:17:29.681 --> 00:17:30.983
if you go to a different location,

00:17:32.418 --> 00:17:34.353
here now we're starting to get a peak for tin,

00:17:35.087 --> 00:17:37.556
indicating the presence of tin on that tooth surface.

00:17:38.757 --> 00:17:42.161
The EDS showed that two of the fragments had traces of copper,

00:17:42.761 --> 00:17:44.630
and all three had traces of tin.

00:17:45.697 --> 00:17:48.934
Because the metals were found only on specific parts of the teeth,

00:17:49.468 --> 00:17:52.071
Housley concluded that they were the remains of fillings,

00:17:52.471 --> 00:17:54.673
not contamination from the metal drum.

00:17:56.642 --> 00:18:00.813
He had already determined from their shape that he had fragments of premolars.

00:18:01.880 --> 00:18:03.982
Now he knew they all had fillings.

00:18:05.150 --> 00:18:06.051
With this information,

00:18:06.351 --> 00:18:10.689
he was ready to compare his findings against Marilyn Garland's dental records.

00:18:11.490 --> 00:18:12.925
So working with a dentist,

00:18:13.525 --> 00:18:14.126
I sat down,

00:18:14.193 --> 00:18:16.829
we went through and figured out which teeth she'd lost in life,

00:18:17.729 --> 00:18:18.864
which teeth had fillings,

00:18:19.698 --> 00:18:20.866
what types of fillings they were,

00:18:20.899 --> 00:18:22.868
and we would prepare a dental chart.

00:18:23.435 --> 00:18:27.039
And this is a basis for comparison with the dental fragments that we had.

00:18:28.240 --> 00:18:31.210
Most of Marilyn Garland's premolars had fillings.

00:18:32.277 --> 00:18:37.449
So the fact that all of the fragments also had fillings was consistent with her dental records.

00:18:38.584 --> 00:18:42.888
So I could never really say that it was absolutely this tooth or that tooth,

00:18:43.355 --> 00:18:45.657
but I could say that she in life had those teeth,

00:18:46.625 --> 00:18:47.559
and of those,

00:18:47.593 --> 00:18:48.894
seven of those eight teeth had fillings,

00:18:48.894 --> 00:18:50.863
and I've got three fragments of those teeth,

00:18:52.397 --> 00:18:53.298
and all of them have fillings.

00:18:53.332 --> 00:18:54.900
So it's a consistency there.

00:18:55.200 --> 00:18:58.470
It's a point that seems to support this association.

00:18:58.804 --> 00:19:00.205
So it's just one bit of evidence.

00:19:01.573 --> 00:19:08.113
Those bits of evidence added up to create a portrait of a victim that was alarmingly similar to Marilyn Garland.

00:19:10.082 --> 00:19:10.949
Marilyn Garland,

00:19:11.383 --> 00:19:12.417
age 35,

00:19:12.784 --> 00:19:17.356
stood 5 feet 2 inches tall and weighed 115 pounds.

00:19:18.657 --> 00:19:21.026
Obviously they couldn't positively say that it was her,

00:19:21.493 --> 00:19:24.263
but he was able to say that it was a petite female,

00:19:24.863 --> 00:19:26.532
possibly white female,

00:19:28.000 --> 00:19:30.235
between the ages of 25 and 35.

00:19:30.235 --> 00:19:30.469
I mean,

00:19:30.502 --> 00:19:31.703
it really narrowed it down,

00:19:31.737 --> 00:19:33.672
which was surprising to me,

00:19:33.772 --> 00:19:35.307
just to find some remains,

00:19:35.374 --> 00:19:36.008
charred remains,

00:19:36.008 --> 00:19:40.612
to be able to look at him and obtain that much information from him.

00:19:40.812 --> 00:19:41.713
That was surprising.

00:19:43.282 --> 00:19:44.116
Awaiting trial,

00:19:44.550 --> 00:19:50.722
Danny King learned that he'd left enough evidence for scientists to come so close to identifying Marilyn Garland.

00:19:51.690 --> 00:19:52.691
Rattled by the news,

00:19:52.958 --> 00:19:54.793
he made one more mistake.

00:19:56.128 --> 00:19:58.497
Danny King confessed a second time,

00:19:59.731 --> 00:20:01.867
this time to an inmate at Summit County Jail,

00:20:02.434 --> 00:20:05.871
and the inmate ended up telling us about it.

00:20:07.439 --> 00:20:12.277
Danny King even told him where he had disposed of the gun.

00:20:14.179 --> 00:20:17.950
King admitted stashing the gun in a storm sewer behind a restaurant.

00:20:18.917 --> 00:20:22.387
Detective Todd drove to the location and found it immediately.

00:20:24.423 --> 00:20:26.058
Police had all they needed.

00:20:26.692 --> 00:20:27.492
The confessions,

00:20:28.093 --> 00:20:29.094
the burned barrel,

00:20:29.895 --> 00:20:30.395
the weapon,

00:20:31.296 --> 00:20:32.297
and Doug Owsley,

00:20:32.798 --> 00:20:37.336
who turned a handful of splintered bones into rock-solid evidence.

00:20:37.970 --> 00:20:39.304
I've been involved in a number of cases.

00:20:39.338 --> 00:20:41.073
cases where you're searching for a missing person.

00:20:41.106 --> 00:20:42.908
You know that person in your heart,

00:20:42.941 --> 00:20:43.842
you know that person's dead.

00:20:44.476 --> 00:20:47.346
And they may even have good strong evidence as to who's behind this.

00:20:47.946 --> 00:20:51.283
But without that body being recovered and without that physical evidence,

00:20:51.750 --> 00:20:52.884
you still could go to trial,

00:20:52.918 --> 00:20:53.986
but it gets much more difficult.

00:20:54.286 --> 00:20:54.620
It gets,

00:20:55.220 --> 00:20:55.420
you know,

00:20:55.454 --> 00:20:57.089
you need a lot of good physical evidence.

00:20:59.391 --> 00:21:01.760
A skeleton or a body is going to be a critical thing.

00:21:03.295 --> 00:21:05.631
Danny King was found guilty of murder.

00:21:06.298 --> 00:21:08.834
He was sentenced to 21 years to life.

00:21:10.902 --> 00:21:15.007
Generations of murderers have relied on fire to hide their crimes.

00:21:16.608 --> 00:21:21.246
But some little detail always lingers for those who know what to look for.

00:21:23.515 --> 00:21:24.216
In Tennessee,

00:21:24.616 --> 00:21:29.655
investigators depended on scorched bones to divine not only a dead man's identity,

00:21:30.322 --> 00:21:33.458
but also the strange circumstances of his death.

00:21:37.129 --> 00:21:38.363
On January 15,

00:21:38.363 --> 00:21:39.865
1981,

00:21:40.232 --> 00:21:42.601
a house burned to the ground in Kingsport,

00:21:42.701 --> 00:21:43.235
Tennessee,

00:21:43.735 --> 00:21:45.570
before the fire department could save it.

00:21:48.140 --> 00:21:48.607
The owner,

00:21:49.007 --> 00:21:49.775
James Grizzle,

00:21:50.142 --> 00:21:51.977
hadn't been seen since the blaze.

00:21:53.011 --> 00:21:54.479
Nobody gave it a second thought,

00:21:54.846 --> 00:21:57.282
because Grizzle spent much of his time in Virginia.

00:22:00.652 --> 00:22:00.852
Then,

00:22:01.253 --> 00:22:06.692
Detective James Moffitt of the Kingsport Police received a call from Grizzle's mother in Virginia.

00:22:07.693 --> 00:22:08.293
What's his name?

00:22:08.560 --> 00:22:09.494
She was concerned,

00:22:09.761 --> 00:22:12.397
because she hadn't heard from her son in several days.

00:22:14.399 --> 00:22:15.734
Moffitt smelled trouble.

00:22:17.836 --> 00:22:23.909
He knew that Grizzle had hired a live-in handyman named Stephen Leon Williams to help renovate the house.

00:22:25.177 --> 00:22:27.512
Williams was not a model citizen.

00:22:28.580 --> 00:22:30.115
I knew Stephen Leon Williams,

00:22:30.649 --> 00:22:33.552
and I was investigating him on some burglaries.

00:22:34.853 --> 00:22:35.420
And I knew that

00:22:36.521 --> 00:22:38.023
Mr. Williams was living with Mr.

00:22:38.056 --> 00:22:40.125
Grizzle in Hawkins County.

00:22:41.159 --> 00:22:41.860
I talked to Mr.

00:22:41.860 --> 00:22:47.065
Grizzle probably about a week before his house burned about Leon.

00:22:48.300 --> 00:22:51.803
The fire department didn't look for a body once the blaze was out.

00:22:52.704 --> 00:22:54.439
But after hearing from Grizzle's mother,

00:22:55.006 --> 00:22:58.143
Moffat began to suspect something more than a house fire.

00:22:59.344 --> 00:23:01.546
If Grizzle was in the house when it burned,

00:23:01.880 --> 00:23:03.582
his remains would still be there.

00:23:05.584 --> 00:23:10.622
Moffat would need an expert to determine if Grizzle's death was an accident or murder.

00:23:13.125 --> 00:23:16.395
He called state forensic anthropologist William Bass.

00:23:17.462 --> 00:23:20.332
It's an interesting case because most houses that have burned down,

00:23:20.465 --> 00:23:21.466
the fire department's been there,

00:23:21.500 --> 00:23:22.868
lots of people have walked through the area.

00:23:23.535 --> 00:23:26.738
This was one of the few cases in which nobody had walked into this house at all.

00:23:26.905 --> 00:23:31.676
So you got there and the house had burned down and there were no footprints in the house at all.

00:23:32.644 --> 00:23:36.281
We were the first ones in there.

00:23:36.982 --> 00:23:42.988
The undisturbed condition of the site assured Bass that any signs of foul play would still be there.

00:23:45.123 --> 00:23:47.559
Moffett pointed out the locations of the bedrooms,

00:23:47.826 --> 00:23:48.160
kitchen,

00:23:48.360 --> 00:23:50.996
and living room so Bass could get his bearings.

00:23:53.598 --> 00:23:55.600
As with any archeological excavation,

00:23:56.001 --> 00:24:00.305
he and his team carefully sifted through the debris with trowels and brushes.

00:24:03.074 --> 00:24:04.810
They moved one brick at a time,

00:24:05.544 --> 00:24:07.779
searching for the all-important remains.

00:24:10.982 --> 00:24:12.951
A little over an hour after they began,

00:24:13.518 --> 00:24:17.756
Bass found some human lower leg bones on the concrete basement floor.

00:24:19.558 --> 00:24:20.125
Before long,

00:24:20.525 --> 00:24:22.894
scattered bones started coming out of the rubble,

00:24:23.395 --> 00:24:23.862
and with them,

00:24:24.329 --> 00:24:26.598
a horrible story began to emerge.

00:24:28.400 --> 00:24:31.369
The body had been lying on its back,

00:24:31.736 --> 00:24:34.072
but the legs were up over the body,

00:24:34.406 --> 00:24:37.142
as if you lay down and take your feet and pull them up to your head.

00:24:37.776 --> 00:24:38.043
However,

00:24:38.043 --> 00:24:38.810
there was no head.

00:24:40.145 --> 00:24:42.347
The victim was separated below the shoulders,

00:24:42.814 --> 00:24:46.318
and the upper and lower halves of the skeleton were 12 feet apart.

00:24:47.285 --> 00:24:48.987
That wasn't entirely unusual.

00:24:49.588 --> 00:24:54.526
A skeleton may become jumbled if the victim had fallen through the floor and landed in the basement.

00:24:56.261 --> 00:24:58.663
But Bass knew that wasn't the case here.

00:24:59.598 --> 00:25:00.532
Had the body fallen,

00:25:00.866 --> 00:25:03.869
he would have found debris between the skeleton and the concrete.

00:25:05.203 --> 00:25:06.505
Bass found no debris.

00:25:07.205 --> 00:25:07.672
In fact,

00:25:08.039 --> 00:25:09.875
the victim was fused to the floor.

00:25:10.742 --> 00:25:13.912
It's like taking a piece of meat and putting it in a hot skillet.

00:25:14.513 --> 00:25:17.516
That meat will sear onto the hot surface.

00:25:17.983 --> 00:25:18.750
In this case,

00:25:19.885 --> 00:25:26.324
the fats running out were so hot that they literally solidified what clothing remained.

00:25:26.791 --> 00:25:34.633
So we had to scrape off the clothing and there was no debris between the floor and the bones.

00:25:36.368 --> 00:25:41.306
The condition of the clothing told Bass the victim was in the basement when the fire began.

00:25:42.874 --> 00:25:44.709
If the body didn't fall from a height,

00:25:45.277 --> 00:25:49.748
Bass knew of only one other way to explain how the bones became scattered.

00:25:50.615 --> 00:25:51.416
An explosion.

00:25:52.684 --> 00:25:53.051
Neighbors'

00:25:53.084 --> 00:25:54.619
reports confirmed Bass'

00:25:54.719 --> 00:25:55.287
conclusion.

00:25:56.154 --> 00:25:58.690
They heard a blast shortly before the fire.

00:26:00.659 --> 00:26:03.094
Judging from the way the upper torso was separated,

00:26:03.728 --> 00:26:07.532
Bass suspected the center of the explosion was on the victim's chest.

00:26:08.633 --> 00:26:10.602
It might have been some freak accident.

00:26:12.137 --> 00:26:14.039
But then Bass found another clue.

00:26:15.140 --> 00:26:18.977
The flattened remains of a spent bullet lay upon the basement floor,

00:26:19.544 --> 00:26:21.046
just below the victim's heart.

00:26:22.414 --> 00:26:28.420
Bass could tell from its mushroom shape that it had traveled through the victim and flattened against the concrete.

00:26:30.789 --> 00:26:31.690
At first glance,

00:26:31.923 --> 00:26:33.258
it looked like homicide.

00:26:34.392 --> 00:26:37.028
But homicide cases are won on hard facts,

00:26:37.395 --> 00:26:38.396
not speculation.

00:26:40.231 --> 00:26:41.132
To prove murder,

00:26:41.499 --> 00:26:43.802
he needed all the evidence he could muster.

00:26:47.038 --> 00:26:49.140
In his lab at the University of Tennessee,

00:26:49.674 --> 00:26:52.978
Bass has teased the truth from countless human remains.

00:26:54.279 --> 00:26:54.512
Here,

00:26:54.813 --> 00:26:58.183
he took a closer look at the bones pulled from Grizzle's house.

00:26:59.384 --> 00:27:04.389
He would try to determine what events caused the destruction of a home and the death of a person.

00:27:07.258 --> 00:27:08.426
With burned remains,

00:27:08.827 --> 00:27:10.195
it's not always easy.

00:27:12.430 --> 00:27:13.498
When a body burns,

00:27:13.898 --> 00:27:15.100
much of it is destroyed.

00:27:15.967 --> 00:27:17.702
The arms and legs separate.

00:27:18.503 --> 00:27:21.973
Fluid in the head turns to steam and bursts the skull.

00:27:24.042 --> 00:27:25.010
As bones burn,

00:27:25.343 --> 00:27:26.478
they retain their shape,

00:27:26.745 --> 00:27:28.847
but they shrink up to 33 percent,

00:27:29.781 --> 00:27:31.383
depending on the temperature of the fire,

00:27:31.883 --> 00:27:33.385
how long they were exposed to it,

00:27:33.818 --> 00:27:35.186
and which bones they are.

00:27:37.155 --> 00:27:38.556
Fire turns them brittle.

00:27:39.290 --> 00:27:40.725
They shatter while they burn,

00:27:41.159 --> 00:27:43.995
and can crumble if they're touched or moved afterward.

00:27:45.230 --> 00:27:48.166
Collecting a complete skeleton is nearly impossible.

00:27:50.235 --> 00:27:52.303
Because burning disfigures bones,

00:27:52.570 --> 00:27:55.140
it's a common way to hide the cause of death.

00:27:56.041 --> 00:28:03.448
Investigators must learn to distinguish the subtle differences between damage caused by fire and damage from a murder weapon.

00:28:05.984 --> 00:28:12.757
Bass demonstrates that even a bullet in the head can go undetected unless the forensic investigator knows how to find it.

00:28:14.292 --> 00:28:17.696
All of these pieces were all together in a jumble.

00:28:18.129 --> 00:28:18.963
We x-rayed this,

00:28:19.297 --> 00:28:23.234
and there is little flakes of lead in the bone,

00:28:24.169 --> 00:28:24.636
indicating,

00:28:24.669 --> 00:28:24.803
hey,

00:28:24.869 --> 00:28:26.071
this individual's been shot.

00:28:26.871 --> 00:28:27.439
Then we wondered,

00:28:28.206 --> 00:28:31.176
can we determine where this individual was shot?

00:28:31.876 --> 00:28:34.679
So we sat down and started putting the skull together,

00:28:35.413 --> 00:28:36.281
all these pieces,

00:28:36.781 --> 00:28:37.415
and sure enough,

00:28:37.782 --> 00:28:39.951
here comes your entry wound right there.

00:28:42.654 --> 00:28:43.455
Joanne Bennett,

00:28:43.655 --> 00:28:44.656
a student of Dr.

00:28:44.689 --> 00:28:44.956
Bass,

00:28:45.323 --> 00:28:49.828
is hoping to find a more efficient way to recognize injuries not caused by fire.

00:28:51.863 --> 00:28:52.664
In her research,

00:28:52.997 --> 00:28:55.366
she experiments with bone scraps from pigs.

00:28:56.534 --> 00:28:56.868
First,

00:28:57.102 --> 00:28:59.437
she damages them to simulate foul play,

00:29:00.271 --> 00:29:02.640
then burns them to see how they change.

00:29:04.109 --> 00:29:12.417
We designed several different traumatic events to address the trauma that anthropologists recognize.

00:29:13.818 --> 00:29:15.954
Bennett and other graduate students saw,

00:29:16.354 --> 00:29:16.788
strike,

00:29:17.455 --> 00:29:19.758
and stab a variety of pig bones.

00:29:21.626 --> 00:29:22.994
The bones are then photographed,

00:29:23.495 --> 00:29:23.895
x-rayed,

00:29:24.028 --> 00:29:27.098
and sketched to record every nuance of the damage.

00:29:29.601 --> 00:29:31.202
Once the bones have been cataloged,

00:29:31.436 --> 00:29:32.437
the students burn them.

00:29:36.574 --> 00:29:37.208
If they're lucky,

00:29:37.542 --> 00:29:40.411
they use a house scheduled to be torched for fire training.

00:29:42.547 --> 00:29:46.851
The goal is to simulate as closely as possible a real-world situation.

00:29:52.390 --> 00:29:53.591
After the smoke clears,

00:29:53.892 --> 00:29:58.563
the students pick through the rubble to retrieve as many bone fragments as they can find.

00:29:59.130 --> 00:29:59.230
Oh,

00:29:59.264 --> 00:30:00.632
I found one.

00:30:00.632 --> 00:30:06.004
They take the bones back to the lab and examine them to see if they can detect the marks they made.

00:30:08.039 --> 00:30:12.443
The marks made by a scalpel or saw blade survived the fire with no problem.

00:30:13.545 --> 00:30:17.215
The fine striations of the saw can even be seen with the naked eye.

00:30:18.716 --> 00:30:21.753
The marks made by the hammer are less easy to discern.

00:30:23.788 --> 00:30:25.657
Bennett is just starting her research.

00:30:26.858 --> 00:30:30.295
As she and her colleagues experiment with a wider sample of bones,

00:30:30.595 --> 00:30:35.867
she feels confident they'll come up with some guidelines forensic investigators can use in the future.

00:30:38.937 --> 00:30:40.371
With the remains in his lab,

00:30:40.738 --> 00:30:45.310
Bass pieced together the sorry events that transpired in James Grizzle's house.

00:30:46.678 --> 00:30:49.814
The flattened bullet and scattered bones suggested murder.

00:30:50.648 --> 00:30:52.750
The color of the bones backed that up.

00:30:54.886 --> 00:30:55.920
As bones heat up,

00:30:56.321 --> 00:30:57.522
their color changes.

00:30:58.323 --> 00:31:02.126
Forensic investigators depend on that to estimate the fire's temperature.

00:31:03.161 --> 00:31:07.565
Especially hot flames suggest an accelerant like gasoline was used.

00:31:08.299 --> 00:31:09.767
And that means arson.

00:31:10.969 --> 00:31:13.605
At about 545 degrees Fahrenheit,

00:31:14.038 --> 00:31:16.474
bones turn reddish or grayish-brown.

00:31:17.675 --> 00:31:20.211
Between 970 and 1200 degrees,

00:31:20.511 --> 00:31:21.379
they turn black.

00:31:22.780 --> 00:31:24.115
Over 1200 degrees,

00:31:24.549 --> 00:31:26.684
all the organic material burns out,

00:31:27.118 --> 00:31:29.087
leaving a white shell of calcium.

00:31:31.256 --> 00:31:33.558
The remains in Grizzle's house were white.

00:31:34.392 --> 00:31:37.128
They must have burned at about 2000 degrees,

00:31:37.762 --> 00:31:39.497
indicating the use of an accelerant.

00:31:40.598 --> 00:31:43.601
Bass concluded the fire was deliberately set.

00:31:46.337 --> 00:31:46.604
Now,

00:31:47.005 --> 00:31:49.507
Bass was sure he could prove homicide.

00:31:50.408 --> 00:31:53.278
The burned remains told him that the victim was shot to death,

00:31:53.912 --> 00:31:55.213
the body blown apart,

00:31:55.813 --> 00:31:58.449
and the house set on fire to hide the crime.

00:31:59.617 --> 00:32:01.019
But who was the victim?

00:32:02.153 --> 00:32:04.455
Though reasonably certain it was James Grizzle,

00:32:05.089 --> 00:32:07.926
Bass had to prove it beyond a reasonable doubt.

00:32:09.027 --> 00:32:10.395
Without naming the victim,

00:32:11.062 --> 00:32:12.997
Moffett couldn't catch his murderer.

00:32:15.533 --> 00:32:16.601
To identify the victim,

00:32:17.068 --> 00:32:19.370
Bass first examined seams in the skull,

00:32:19.871 --> 00:32:20.838
called sutures.

00:32:22.140 --> 00:32:26.044
They slowly close as we grow older and are gone by age 40.

00:32:27.345 --> 00:32:28.813
Inside the skull of the victim,

00:32:29.213 --> 00:32:30.615
the sutures had disappeared.

00:32:31.783 --> 00:32:32.550
On the outside,

00:32:32.850 --> 00:32:34.953
they were almost completely gone as well.

00:32:36.421 --> 00:32:38.790
This would indicate that he was not only 18,

00:32:39.290 --> 00:32:39.724
but was,

00:32:40.558 --> 00:32:40.825
you know,

00:32:41.159 --> 00:32:41.793
middle-aged.

00:32:42.026 --> 00:32:43.194
You get at least 30,

00:32:43.661 --> 00:32:44.128
so forth.

00:32:45.063 --> 00:32:46.764
Age can be determined another way,

00:32:47.398 --> 00:32:49.767
by looking at the joint surfaces of the bones.

00:32:50.702 --> 00:32:51.536
As people age,

00:32:51.836 --> 00:32:54.405
the body deposits more bone around the joints,

00:32:54.906 --> 00:32:57.108
causing a condition called arthritic lipping.

00:32:58.276 --> 00:32:59.344
It begins in the late

00:32:59.777 --> 00:33:02.480
20s or early 30s and increases with age.

00:33:04.015 --> 00:33:06.985
The lipping was consistent with someone in his mid-30s.

00:33:08.419 --> 00:33:11.089
James Grizzle was 38 years old.

00:33:12.890 --> 00:33:16.060
Bass next examined the external occipital protuberance,

00:33:16.561 --> 00:33:18.162
the bulge on the back of the head,

00:33:18.563 --> 00:33:19.964
to determine the victim's sex.

00:33:21.232 --> 00:33:24.035
The structure is larger in males than in females.

00:33:25.136 --> 00:33:29.040
It left Bass with little room for doubt that the victim was male.

00:33:30.208 --> 00:33:32.276
This is one of the best ones I've ever seen.

00:33:32.343 --> 00:33:37.448
This is a classic textbook case of external occipital protuberance,

00:33:37.782 --> 00:33:40.718
which would indicate that this individual was a male.

00:33:41.319 --> 00:33:42.754
So by just this little piece,

00:33:42.787 --> 00:33:43.955
you could get not only sex,

00:33:44.088 --> 00:33:46.424
but age with that right there.

00:33:48.092 --> 00:33:48.826
From the evidence,

00:33:49.193 --> 00:33:52.897
Bass determined the victim was a white male in his mid-30s,

00:33:53.531 --> 00:33:55.867
a description consistent with James Grizzle,

00:33:56.567 --> 00:33:59.070
but not close enough to hold up in court.

00:34:00.638 --> 00:34:02.106
To be absolutely positive,

00:34:02.573 --> 00:34:05.076
Bass relied on Grizzle's dental records.

00:34:05.977 --> 00:34:10.481
You have this tooth with a filling that's attached to the tooth after the fire.

00:34:11.282 --> 00:34:16.587
So we asked the police if they could find from the family a dental record of him.

00:34:16.654 --> 00:34:19.524
We knew he'd been to the dentist because you could see there were fillings in here.

00:34:20.324 --> 00:34:23.728
Bass compared the structure of the tooth and the shape of the root.

00:34:24.495 --> 00:34:25.997
From Grizzle's dental records,

00:34:26.230 --> 00:34:28.699
he was able to make his final determination.

00:34:29.434 --> 00:34:32.003
This was the skeleton of James Grizzle.

00:34:34.272 --> 00:34:38.676
The teeth provided Bass with a quick and easy way to make a difficult identification.

00:34:39.844 --> 00:34:43.848
But it was important that he first tried to make the ID using the bones.

00:34:44.949 --> 00:34:45.483
Otherwise,

00:34:45.750 --> 00:34:49.620
he'd have no way of knowing if more than one victim was buried in the rubble.

00:34:51.322 --> 00:34:54.492
If the bones and teeth all suggest the same person,

00:34:55.326 --> 00:34:58.496
Bass can be confident only one victim was buried there.

00:35:00.932 --> 00:35:03.000
That person was James Grizzle.

00:35:06.270 --> 00:35:07.505
Based on this information,

00:35:07.872 --> 00:35:10.908
Detective Moffitt had a case against Leon Williams.

00:35:12.009 --> 00:35:18.182
It was further bolstered by the discovery that Williams had stolen James Grizzle's truck and forged his checks.

00:35:20.485 --> 00:35:21.486
Faced with the evidence,

00:35:21.786 --> 00:35:23.287
Leon Williams confessed.

00:35:25.456 --> 00:35:26.224
In his confession,

00:35:26.457 --> 00:35:27.658
he confirmed Bass'

00:35:27.825 --> 00:35:29.660
careful reconstruction of the crime.

00:35:31.462 --> 00:35:33.464
He shot James Grizzle in the basement,

00:35:34.665 --> 00:35:36.467
poured gasoline throughout the house,

00:35:37.201 --> 00:35:38.703
strapped an explosive to the victim,

00:35:39.470 --> 00:35:40.338
and when it went off,

00:35:40.771 --> 00:35:42.273
it set the house on fire.

00:35:45.276 --> 00:35:46.043
For his crime,

00:35:46.511 --> 00:35:48.980
Leon Williams was sentenced to life.

00:35:50.715 --> 00:35:52.016
If you don't destroy the evidence,

00:35:52.016 --> 00:35:55.453
if you don't bring in a bulldozer and clean it all off or something like that,

00:35:55.453 --> 00:35:56.454
the evidence is there.

00:35:57.121 --> 00:35:58.055
If you know what to look for,

00:35:58.055 --> 00:35:59.056
it takes you a while.

00:36:00.158 --> 00:36:09.800
But we were able to reconstruct the events that occurred in this case exactly the way the individual who was charged and convicted said it happened.

00:36:11.035 --> 00:36:15.907
William Bass unmasked a killer who went to great lengths to disguise his crime.

00:36:17.608 --> 00:36:18.075
Elsewhere,

00:36:18.442 --> 00:36:24.048
a man went to greater lengths to bring the bones of his missing brother back from Guatemala.

00:36:27.118 --> 00:36:27.585
Guatemala,

00:36:27.585 --> 00:36:29.420
1985.

00:36:30.855 --> 00:36:35.326
A country caught in a civil war between the government and communist guerrillas.

00:36:38.496 --> 00:36:39.697
In the outlying villages,

00:36:40.031 --> 00:36:41.999
the government appoints civil patrollers,

00:36:43.301 --> 00:36:45.736
an armed militia made up of civilians.

00:36:46.938 --> 00:36:49.006
Everyone must serve one day a week.

00:36:50.708 --> 00:36:52.210
The government provides guns,

00:36:52.510 --> 00:36:53.344
but no training.

00:36:55.279 --> 00:36:58.282
It instructs the militia to patrol for communist rebels.

00:36:59.750 --> 00:37:01.786
But nobody patrols the militia.

00:37:03.588 --> 00:37:05.056
For hungry young journalists,

00:37:05.056 --> 00:37:09.961
1985 was an exciting and dangerous time to be in Guatemala.

00:37:12.096 --> 00:37:18.369
Freelance American journalist Nick Blake and photographer Griffith Davis underestimated that danger.

00:37:20.738 --> 00:37:21.906
In their pursuit of a story,

00:37:22.440 --> 00:37:26.043
they headed into a remote part of the country and vanished.

00:37:29.614 --> 00:37:30.581
Nick Blake's brother,

00:37:30.715 --> 00:37:31.048
Randy,

00:37:31.382 --> 00:37:33.884
did all he could to uncover what happened.

00:37:35.353 --> 00:37:37.888
My brother disappeared in March of 85.

00:37:37.888 --> 00:37:43.661
I'd been doing requests with the State Department and four or five other federal agencies who would be in a position to know things,

00:37:43.694 --> 00:37:44.395
such as the CIA.

00:37:45.596 --> 00:37:48.633
and the National Security Agency and others for ever since,

00:37:48.633 --> 00:37:48.933
you know,

00:37:49.367 --> 00:37:51.402
probably 1987.

00:37:51.936 --> 00:37:54.338
Randy Blake found only dead ends.

00:37:55.172 --> 00:37:55.406
Then,

00:37:55.706 --> 00:37:57.275
a year and a half into his search,

00:37:57.642 --> 00:38:02.179
he heard an unconfirmed story from an American teacher in a village in Guatemala.

00:38:03.147 --> 00:38:05.349
We would go to little fiestas with him and so forth,

00:38:05.349 --> 00:38:06.083
and he got to know them,

00:38:06.117 --> 00:38:09.654
and they would talk about the gringos that got killed by the Civil Patrol.

00:38:14.492 --> 00:38:16.027
Piecing together the details,

00:38:16.327 --> 00:38:20.831
the teacher believed the civil patrol shot the journalists in 1985.

00:38:22.667 --> 00:38:25.102
A ravine became their makeshift grave.

00:38:28.739 --> 00:38:31.142
The Blake family kept pressure on the U.S.

00:38:31.208 --> 00:38:33.544
and Guatemalan governments to find Nick.

00:38:36.514 --> 00:38:37.448
To hide the crime,

00:38:37.815 --> 00:38:42.553
the Civil Patrol retrieved the skeletons and burned them in a raging bonfire.

00:38:47.925 --> 00:38:51.662
The flames split and shattered the bones beyond all recognition.

00:38:52.630 --> 00:38:54.765
Or at least that's what the patrollers thought.

00:38:56.500 --> 00:38:58.369
But the Blake family persisted.

00:38:59.170 --> 00:39:02.807
The patrollers were pressured into sending the remains to the United States.

00:39:06.977 --> 00:39:08.479
They arrived in 1990,

00:39:09.113 --> 00:39:11.382
but an analysis uncovered a deception.

00:39:12.116 --> 00:39:14.852
The crate did not contain human remains.

00:39:17.121 --> 00:39:18.856
Convinced they had exposed a cover-up,

00:39:19.423 --> 00:39:20.491
the Blakes and U.S.

00:39:20.558 --> 00:39:22.226
authorities demanded the truth.

00:39:24.862 --> 00:39:25.663
Felipe Alva,

00:39:25.996 --> 00:39:28.666
the regional commander of the Guatemalan Civil Patrol,

00:39:29.033 --> 00:39:30.701
refused to talk about the incident,

00:39:31.469 --> 00:39:33.804
until the Blakes offered an incentive.

00:39:34.872 --> 00:39:37.141
And he was going to be offered a $10,000 reward.

00:39:38.209 --> 00:39:38.509
And so,

00:39:38.876 --> 00:39:42.880
but it was going to be paid to him in allotments as he performed along the job.

00:39:47.852 --> 00:39:48.919
Enticed by the offer,

00:39:49.387 --> 00:39:52.256
Alva agreed to send the real remains to the U.S.

00:39:52.623 --> 00:39:55.192
so long as the family promised not to prosecute.

00:39:56.560 --> 00:40:00.131
He came out with a partial unearthing of remains from the site,

00:40:00.998 --> 00:40:02.700
and he brought them back in two boxes,

00:40:02.800 --> 00:40:04.435
very curious things.

00:40:04.468 --> 00:40:06.036
They were essentially little caskets,

00:40:06.137 --> 00:40:06.971
mini caskets,

00:40:07.872 --> 00:40:11.075
square pine boxes that were velvet-lined.

00:40:11.842 --> 00:40:20.217
And it was the strangest thing because they had dirt in them from the site with little bone chips salted throughout them and so forth because that's essentially what was remaining of my brother and his friend.

00:40:27.458 --> 00:40:34.064
The grim homecoming occurred without ceremony at a loading dock behind the Smithsonian Institution in Washington,

00:40:34.098 --> 00:40:34.365
D.C.

00:40:36.700 --> 00:40:40.638
The unmarked remains had traveled over 3,000 miles to get there,

00:40:44.708 --> 00:40:47.878
navigating through a maze of deception and lies.

00:40:52.383 --> 00:40:52.516
But...

00:40:52.550 --> 00:40:58.656
But more work lay ahead before the Blakes could put their loved one to rest and find the closure they sought.

00:41:00.925 --> 00:41:01.492
This time,

00:41:01.559 --> 00:41:04.528
they had to be certain the bones in the crates were truly

00:41:04.895 --> 00:41:05.996
Nick's and Griffith's.

00:41:08.098 --> 00:41:10.668
For the delicate task of identifying the remains,

00:41:10.968 --> 00:41:13.504
they pinned their hopes on forensic anthropologist

00:41:13.871 --> 00:41:14.572
Doug Owsley.

00:41:16.140 --> 00:41:17.274
When the crates arrived,

00:41:17.441 --> 00:41:21.579
they were x-rayed to see if they required special handling to avoid damage.

00:41:27.051 --> 00:41:28.686
They contained no surprises,

00:41:30.521 --> 00:41:31.288
mostly soil,

00:41:31.755 --> 00:41:31.989
ash,

00:41:32.356 --> 00:41:32.690
roots,

00:41:32.957 --> 00:41:33.891
and bits of bone.

00:41:39.864 --> 00:41:40.064
Then,

00:41:40.397 --> 00:41:42.466
Owsley and his team sorted the bones.

00:41:44.034 --> 00:41:46.704
It was immediately apparent the remains were human.

00:41:47.905 --> 00:41:51.342
They also found a duplication of parts and a color difference.

00:41:52.943 --> 00:41:56.046
Owsley was certain these were the remains of two people.

00:41:57.715 --> 00:42:00.551
Out of 150 pounds of dirt and debris,

00:42:00.985 --> 00:42:03.754
they recovered about two pounds of incinerated bone.

00:42:04.755 --> 00:42:09.293
The largest of the more than 1,600 fragments was just three inches long.

00:42:12.897 --> 00:42:14.164
But Owsley was fortunate.

00:42:14.999 --> 00:42:18.769
Some of the fragments came from the occipital protuberance at the back of the skull.

00:42:19.770 --> 00:42:23.307
The large size of the specimens indicated both were male.

00:42:24.942 --> 00:42:27.144
But they could have been almost any two men.

00:42:28.479 --> 00:42:32.783
Then he found a nickel-sized piece of bone that made all the difference.

00:42:34.718 --> 00:42:40.691
It was a fragment that fits in the forehead and forms a ridge or crest for the sinuses inside the skull.

00:42:41.759 --> 00:42:43.394
It's called the frontal crest.

00:42:44.194 --> 00:42:47.298
Now this is a really unusual configuration of the frontal crest here.

00:42:47.431 --> 00:42:49.166
Usually this is a solid...

00:42:49.700 --> 00:42:50.534
prominent ridge,

00:42:50.935 --> 00:42:52.770
but in a small percentage of the population,

00:42:52.803 --> 00:42:55.706
you can get a double ridge morphology like that.

00:42:55.806 --> 00:42:56.740
So we had this piece,

00:42:57.041 --> 00:42:57.908
and based on that,

00:42:58.509 --> 00:43:03.747
I had a microfilm of an X-ray that was taken of Griffith Davis.

00:43:05.149 --> 00:43:08.052
Owsley compared the crest with an X-ray of Davis'

00:43:08.152 --> 00:43:10.020
head taken after a car accident.

00:43:10.955 --> 00:43:14.992
The unusual structure of the bone fragment from Guatemala matched the X-ray.

00:43:16.360 --> 00:43:19.096
But did that prove the fragment came from Davis'

00:43:19.129 --> 00:43:19.563
skull?

00:43:21.165 --> 00:43:24.768
Not unless Owsley could first prove that its structure was truly rare.

00:43:27.705 --> 00:43:31.141
To see how often the crest structure occurred in the general population,

00:43:31.809 --> 00:43:35.279
Owsley referred to a group of skeletons known as the Terry Collection.

00:43:37.781 --> 00:43:43.020
The more than 1,600 skeletons of the Terry Collection were donated for medical research from the

00:43:43.854 --> 00:43:44.221
1920s to the

00:43:45.122 --> 00:43:45.255
1940s.

00:43:46.223 --> 00:43:48.425
They represent a cross-section of the population.

00:43:49.660 --> 00:43:52.830
The medical history of each individual is part of the record.

00:43:55.666 --> 00:44:00.571
Owsley used the Terry collection to see what percentage of the population shared Davis'

00:44:00.671 --> 00:44:01.939
frontal crest structure.

00:44:03.874 --> 00:44:06.543
If only a few people had that bone configuration,

00:44:07.044 --> 00:44:10.080
and if it was shared by the Guatemala sample and Davis,

00:44:10.547 --> 00:44:14.618
then Owsley could reasonably conclude the bones could belong to Davis.

00:44:15.819 --> 00:44:18.222
If you look at this cranial vault,

00:44:18.255 --> 00:44:18.722
for instance,

00:44:19.156 --> 00:44:21.725
one of the things that you've got is here's a cribriform plate.

00:44:22.426 --> 00:44:23.193
foramen cecum,

00:44:23.627 --> 00:44:26.096
and then you've got the standard type of frontal crest.

00:44:26.864 --> 00:44:29.667
And it's a very prominent ridge that occurs in 88%

00:44:29.667 --> 00:44:30.434
of white males.

00:44:31.769 --> 00:44:31.935
Now,

00:44:31.935 --> 00:44:32.836
in contrast,

00:44:33.137 --> 00:44:33.971
you see how you've got,

00:44:34.004 --> 00:44:34.371
again,

00:44:35.139 --> 00:44:35.906
the same structure,

00:44:35.906 --> 00:44:37.875
but now we've got a double ridge and a groove.

00:44:38.642 --> 00:44:42.312
This is the type that we see in Griffith Davis's cranium.

00:44:42.913 --> 00:44:44.281
This occurs 9%

00:44:44.281 --> 00:44:44.882
of the time.

00:44:46.483 --> 00:44:51.422
Davis and the remains from Guatemala shared the same rare anatomical feature.

00:44:52.389 --> 00:44:55.693
Owsley felt confident the skull fragments could belong to Davis.

00:44:57.261 --> 00:44:59.530
But were the remaining bones Nick Blake's?

00:45:01.165 --> 00:45:04.768
The limited sample enabled Owsley to perform only one more test,

00:45:05.469 --> 00:45:07.671
determining the relative age of the bodies.

00:45:09.139 --> 00:45:09.573
For this,

00:45:09.640 --> 00:45:11.709
he relied on the sutures in the skulls.

00:45:13.911 --> 00:45:16.513
In the specimen that Owsley thought to be Griffith Davis,

00:45:16.914 --> 00:45:18.649
the suture showed advanced closure,

00:45:19.216 --> 00:45:20.317
suggesting a man in his

00:45:20.751 --> 00:45:20.884
30s.

00:45:21.919 --> 00:45:24.154
The sutures on the other remains were still open,

00:45:24.755 --> 00:45:26.056
indicating a younger man.

00:45:27.958 --> 00:45:29.259
So that helped differentiate.

00:45:29.259 --> 00:45:31.462
The two males were differing in age by about

00:45:32.029 --> 00:45:32.663
11 years.

00:45:33.430 --> 00:45:35.966
And this helped me determine that this is the older person.

00:45:37.334 --> 00:45:38.335
At the time of his death,

00:45:38.702 --> 00:45:40.237
Griffith Davis was 38.

00:45:40.971 --> 00:45:42.606
Nick Blake was 27.

00:45:43.607 --> 00:45:46.076
Owsley's calculation was right on the mark.

00:45:47.711 --> 00:45:48.612
To Randy Blake,

00:45:49.046 --> 00:45:51.014
Owsley's identification was encouraging,

00:45:51.348 --> 00:45:53.117
but naggingly inconclusive.

00:45:54.251 --> 00:45:56.887
If these bones were truly the remains of his brother,

00:45:57.354 --> 00:45:59.456
he needed to be absolutely sure.

00:46:00.557 --> 00:46:02.593
As long as a shadow of doubt persisted,

00:46:02.893 --> 00:46:04.928
the Blakes could not give up their search.

00:46:05.996 --> 00:46:07.631
But Owsley had reached his limit.

00:46:08.265 --> 00:46:11.135
He could go no further without more remains.

00:46:12.803 --> 00:46:13.637
We said to ourselves,

00:46:13.670 --> 00:46:13.937
you know,

00:46:14.738 --> 00:46:15.038
I mean,

00:46:15.372 --> 00:46:16.206
it's probably Nick,

00:46:16.974 --> 00:46:17.207
but,

00:46:17.508 --> 00:46:17.741
you know,

00:46:17.808 --> 00:46:18.175
darn it,

00:46:18.375 --> 00:46:24.014
we want to get up to that site and we want to determine definitely that this is Nick and let's just make a trip down there.

00:46:24.047 --> 00:46:24.982
We've got an opportunity.

00:46:26.250 --> 00:46:31.488
Going to Guatemala was the only choice the Blakes had if they wanted to honor their brother.

00:46:32.689 --> 00:46:34.258
But Owsley had to accompany them.

00:46:35.159 --> 00:46:39.196
Only he could provide the assurance the Blakes had waited so long for.

00:46:40.631 --> 00:46:41.064
Together,

00:46:41.532 --> 00:46:46.436
they would retrace the final steps of Nicholas Blake and Griffith Davis.

00:46:50.641 --> 00:46:53.110
With forensic anthropologist Doug Owsley's help,

00:46:53.644 --> 00:46:56.580
Randy Blake felt he was close to finding his brother.

00:46:57.815 --> 00:47:02.219
He knew that the only way to claim Nick's remains was to retrieve them himself.

00:47:08.392 --> 00:47:08.692
Randy,

00:47:09.226 --> 00:47:10.060
his brother Sam,

00:47:10.661 --> 00:47:11.028
Owsley,

00:47:11.261 --> 00:47:15.766
and a colleague made the trip to Guatemala in June 1992.

00:47:17.501 --> 00:47:20.871
There they met Regional Civil Patrol Commander Felipe Alva.

00:47:21.905 --> 00:47:24.875
He took them to the remote mountain location of the remains.

00:47:26.076 --> 00:47:27.644
But Owsley wasn't buying it.

00:47:28.979 --> 00:47:32.082
I take my trowel and do a little scraping.

00:47:32.182 --> 00:47:35.586
I cut down to about 15 centimeters and I come up and say,

00:47:35.586 --> 00:47:36.386
this is the wrong site.

00:47:36.386 --> 00:47:37.821
I say there's not enough ash,

00:47:38.322 --> 00:47:39.656
there's no bone fragments here,

00:47:40.557 --> 00:47:42.793
there's none of the red soil that I'm looking for.

00:47:43.327 --> 00:47:48.332
I just looked at all of these two containers a couple months before and there's no...

00:47:50.467 --> 00:47:52.169
None of the roots that were found in any of that.

00:47:53.136 --> 00:47:53.370
Then,

00:47:53.770 --> 00:47:54.338
sheepishly,

00:47:54.838 --> 00:48:02.646
Alva's assistant reached under a log and removed a green plastic bag that contained a handful of human bone fragments and some soil.

00:48:03.981 --> 00:48:05.315
And in this 8 ounces of soil,

00:48:05.382 --> 00:48:06.416
it's got the right color,

00:48:06.416 --> 00:48:07.317
it's got the charcoal,

00:48:07.317 --> 00:48:08.218
it's got root fragments,

00:48:08.452 --> 00:48:08.785
and I say,

00:48:08.819 --> 00:48:09.887
this is from the right site,

00:48:09.887 --> 00:48:10.187
and he says,

00:48:10.220 --> 00:48:10.988
I got it right here.

00:48:12.256 --> 00:48:15.058
The assistant told them these were the last of the remains.

00:48:15.926 --> 00:48:18.395
The Americans demanded to see where they came from.

00:48:19.696 --> 00:48:22.266
We went back to our little hotel in Nebab,

00:48:22.399 --> 00:48:24.201
and we basically grilled,

00:48:24.401 --> 00:48:24.701
I mean,

00:48:24.868 --> 00:48:29.373
grilled Felipe Alva for three hours that night with a Guatemalan colonel,

00:48:30.007 --> 00:48:30.173
U.S.

00:48:30.240 --> 00:48:31.408
Embassy defense attache,

00:48:31.642 --> 00:48:31.842
U.S.

00:48:31.909 --> 00:48:32.509
Army colonel,

00:48:32.809 --> 00:48:34.111
a couple of other people in the room,

00:48:34.111 --> 00:48:34.845
and basically said to him,

00:48:34.845 --> 00:48:35.078
I said,

00:48:35.145 --> 00:48:35.312
Alva,

00:48:35.345 --> 00:48:36.113
you lied to us.

00:48:37.848 --> 00:48:38.515
Under duress,

00:48:39.049 --> 00:48:44.821
Felipe Alva agreed to take the team to a site he swore was the actual location of the remains.

00:48:46.089 --> 00:48:47.824
When they arrived two days later,

00:48:48.425 --> 00:48:50.594
Owsley looked it over with a skeptic's eye.

00:48:52.296 --> 00:48:54.998
We could see the indentation where the soil had been taken out.

00:48:55.032 --> 00:48:56.300
We worked it around the perimeters,

00:48:56.600 --> 00:48:57.601
and we found a lot of things.

00:48:57.634 --> 00:48:59.503
One of the things we found lots of was metal.

00:48:59.703 --> 00:48:59.870
Well,

00:48:59.870 --> 00:49:01.371
here's part of an eyeglass frame,

00:49:01.705 --> 00:49:03.540
part of the eyeglass piece in the frame,

00:49:03.540 --> 00:49:05.909
and it's a circular type of pattern.

00:49:05.943 --> 00:49:08.045
And this is one of the things that when we first picked it up,

00:49:08.478 --> 00:49:10.180
one of the brothers of this man said,

00:49:10.180 --> 00:49:11.415
that looks like his glasses.

00:49:12.382 --> 00:49:14.918
So we knew that we were in a very significant spot.

00:49:15.886 --> 00:49:17.821
Now certain they had the right spot,

00:49:18.288 --> 00:49:21.158
the team collected all they could and headed home.

00:49:22.759 --> 00:49:24.661
It was almost like tailor-made for Doug Owles.

00:49:24.695 --> 00:49:24.928
I mean,

00:49:24.928 --> 00:49:27.931
it was like it was so perfect because...

00:49:29.666 --> 00:49:29.766
He,

00:49:30.767 --> 00:49:31.068
you know,

00:49:31.268 --> 00:49:33.370
we were so lucky to have him in that one day,

00:49:33.904 --> 00:49:37.908
because clearly a deception was planned and was being executed,

00:49:38.375 --> 00:49:38.709
clearly.

00:49:39.910 --> 00:49:47.150
And he just totally blew it out of the water by being there and allowed us to have the ammunition to get this guy to come clean.

00:49:49.286 --> 00:49:54.057
Among the bones Doug Owsley carried back with him were fragments of a shattered jawbone.

00:49:55.258 --> 00:49:58.161
They still held some of the teeth and all the roots.

00:49:59.563 --> 00:50:03.633
They would enable him to make a direct comparison to Blake's dental records.

00:50:05.302 --> 00:50:07.604
From the dental records that we had of him in life,

00:50:08.538 --> 00:50:17.014
we knew that he had impacted third molars and they had come in at an angle where the third molar had bumped into the second and had gotten locked up.

00:50:17.547 --> 00:50:22.185
And so what it had actually done is wore a contact or a wear facet into the side of the second molar.

00:50:22.185 --> 00:50:22.919
Thank you.

00:50:23.687 --> 00:50:27.190
And it had a very distinctive root associated with it.

00:50:29.659 --> 00:50:31.395
From the shape of the teeth and roots,

00:50:31.895 --> 00:50:35.032
Owsley was able to positively identify Nick Blake.

00:50:36.400 --> 00:50:37.334
For the Blake family,

00:50:37.801 --> 00:50:40.837
it marked the end of a grueling emotional ordeal.

00:50:42.539 --> 00:50:47.144
Now they could grieve Nick's loss and give his remains a proper burial.

00:50:48.412 --> 00:50:49.112
That meant everything,

00:50:49.112 --> 00:50:55.485
to repatriate his remains and bring them back and bury them in a cemetery and give him a grave marker,

00:50:55.852 --> 00:50:56.353
which we did.

00:50:58.722 --> 00:51:03.226
Not enough remains of Blake or Davis were recovered to confirm they were shot.

00:51:04.361 --> 00:51:05.796
For the Blakes and the Davises,

00:51:06.196 --> 00:51:07.864
that was no longer important.

00:51:09.633 --> 00:51:12.536
Bringing Nick and Griffith home was enough.

00:51:15.772 --> 00:51:19.076
From the ashes of murder rise lingering clues,

00:51:20.677 --> 00:51:23.046
transformed but never erased.

00:51:25.215 --> 00:51:27.484
As scientists learn to read their meaning,

00:51:27.984 --> 00:51:30.420
they can restore what the fire has taken.

00:51:31.955 --> 00:51:32.789
And by doing so,

00:51:33.490 --> 00:51:36.393
find a loved one or catch a killer.

00:51:44.234 --> 00:51:48.638
Investigators seek a serial bomber whose targets crisscross New York State.

00:51:50.307 --> 00:51:55.579
Four deadly explosions in 90 minutes leave only vague clues to the bomber's identity.

00:52:02.119 --> 00:52:06.590
Detectives in Florida rush to the scene of a massive explosion at a lavish estate.

00:52:10.494 --> 00:52:14.564
Somewhere in the twisted wreckage lie clues that will lead them to the killer.

00:52:24.241 --> 00:52:28.912
Bomb investigators must piece together the shattered remains of an intricate puzzle.

00:52:31.014 --> 00:52:36.453
Every long and grueling bombing investigation begins with a short fuse.

00:53:01.511 --> 00:53:03.079
Eleanor Fowler of West Valley,

00:53:03.146 --> 00:53:03.547
New York,

00:53:03.813 --> 00:53:10.754
had no idea the phone call she received a few days after Christmas in 1993 would be a prelude to death.

00:53:11.721 --> 00:53:16.026
A delivery service asked for directions to her house so they could deliver a package.

00:53:27.070 --> 00:53:27.637
Suspicious,

00:53:28.205 --> 00:53:29.506
Eleanor called the police.

00:53:30.607 --> 00:53:33.076
They told her anonymous packages aren't a crime,

00:53:33.376 --> 00:53:34.511
and there was nothing they could do.

00:53:36.479 --> 00:53:38.915
Eleanor accepted the box with reluctance.

00:53:45.655 --> 00:53:48.425
She cut into it and removed the crumpled newspaper.

00:53:55.332 --> 00:53:55.899
Inside,

00:53:55.899 --> 00:53:57.000
she found a toolbox.

00:53:58.001 --> 00:53:59.903
Whoever sent it didn't include a note.

00:54:06.042 --> 00:54:07.911
She flipped the latch and lifted the lid,

00:54:08.745 --> 00:54:10.347
triggering a fatal explosion.

00:54:26.696 --> 00:54:31.501
Eleanor Fowler was the first victim in what was to become a statewide serial bombing.

00:54:33.403 --> 00:54:34.437
Five more bombs,

00:54:34.604 --> 00:54:36.773
concealed inside identical toolboxes,

00:54:37.073 --> 00:54:40.777
were delivered throughout northern and western New York within 90 minutes.

00:54:42.379 --> 00:54:43.780
Four of the bombs exploded,

00:54:44.247 --> 00:54:46.216
killing five and injuring two.

00:54:49.919 --> 00:54:53.323
Investigators from three New York offices of the Bureau of Alcohol,

00:54:53.423 --> 00:54:53.790
Tobacco,

00:54:53.857 --> 00:54:54.658
and Firearms,

00:54:55.025 --> 00:54:56.760
along with their National Response Team,

00:54:57.227 --> 00:55:00.096
followed the trail of explosions to each bomb site.

00:55:01.331 --> 00:55:05.101
The ATF is the federal agency assigned to investigate bombings.

00:55:20.350 --> 00:55:26.056
Special Agent Scott Sammas of the Rochester Bureau of the ATF was one of the first agents to respond.

00:55:27.957 --> 00:55:29.859
The investigation starts on December 28th,

00:55:29.859 --> 00:55:31.494
1993,

00:55:31.494 --> 00:55:33.496
when I receive a phone call from my supervisor,

00:55:33.697 --> 00:55:35.832
who tells me to respond to Buffalo.

00:55:36.066 --> 00:55:37.334
He's had several bombings,

00:55:37.567 --> 00:55:38.601
possibly several deaths.

00:55:39.135 --> 00:55:40.236
Please come out there and assist.

00:55:41.037 --> 00:55:41.771
I'm in Rochester.

00:55:41.871 --> 00:55:43.340
I'm an hour and a half away.

00:55:45.275 --> 00:55:53.283
He then calls me back a few minutes later and asks me to stay in Rochester because there's just been a detonation in Rochester with possibly some deaths there.

00:56:01.257 --> 00:56:07.063
The four simultaneous bombings would stretch the limits of the ATF and other law enforcement agencies.

00:56:09.833 --> 00:56:10.533
At each scene,

00:56:10.834 --> 00:56:11.468
investigators'

00:56:11.534 --> 00:56:14.170
first task was to find every piece of evidence,

00:56:14.471 --> 00:56:15.572
no matter how small,

00:56:15.939 --> 00:56:18.174
no matter how inconsequential it looked.

00:56:20.210 --> 00:56:25.448
It was too soon to tell what might point them to the person who sent the deadly packages.

00:56:27.183 --> 00:56:31.621
Much of the evidence consisted of small fragments coated with explosive residue.

00:56:32.622 --> 00:56:34.991
To determine what kind of explosive was used,

00:56:35.258 --> 00:56:42.332
the evidence at each scene was bagged and sent to forensic chemist Doug Klapek at the ATF Laboratories in Rockville,

00:56:42.365 --> 00:56:42.932
Maryland.

00:56:43.199 --> 00:56:43.366
Well,

00:56:43.366 --> 00:56:44.067
in this case,

00:56:44.134 --> 00:56:48.071
we had to make sure that everything not only was stored separately up in Rochester,

00:56:48.138 --> 00:56:50.273
but was shipped separately down here.

00:56:51.141 --> 00:56:53.676
and different people were handling different parts of evidence.

00:56:53.743 --> 00:56:55.645
Even though things were already in airtight containers,

00:56:55.645 --> 00:56:57.580
we wanted to make sure that was the case.

00:57:00.483 --> 00:57:02.152
As the lab processed the evidence,

00:57:02.485 --> 00:57:04.621
investigators tried to trace the packages.

00:57:05.588 --> 00:57:08.625
The bomber used local delivery services and the U.S.

00:57:08.691 --> 00:57:08.992
mail,

00:57:09.526 --> 00:57:11.528
making the parcels difficult to trace.

00:57:13.630 --> 00:57:15.265
Unaware of their deadly cargo,

00:57:15.732 --> 00:57:18.268
the unsuspecting drivers made their rounds.

00:57:24.174 --> 00:57:24.774
The packages,

00:57:25.108 --> 00:57:26.776
weighing about 16 pounds,

00:57:27.143 --> 00:57:28.178
looked harmless enough.

00:57:29.245 --> 00:57:32.449
How could the delivery people know they were laden with death?

00:57:37.620 --> 00:57:39.622
Not all the bombs reached their targets.

00:57:40.323 --> 00:57:45.728
When one driver tried to deliver a package to Corrections Officer Scott Kemp at the New York State Penitentiary,

00:57:46.162 --> 00:57:47.697
he learned Kemp wasn't working.

00:57:48.565 --> 00:57:50.600
The penitentiary couldn't accept the package.

00:57:54.604 --> 00:57:58.241
Aggravated at the delay it would cause him to re-deliver it another time,

00:57:58.641 --> 00:58:00.810
he threw the box into the back of his van.

00:58:02.779 --> 00:58:03.680
Through sheer luck,

00:58:04.080 --> 00:58:05.582
the bomb didn't detonate.

00:58:08.184 --> 00:58:13.623
This bomb and another that malfunctioned were disarmed by the Erie County Sheriff's bomb squad.

00:58:16.092 --> 00:58:18.995
They provided investigators with an important break in the case.

00:58:20.363 --> 00:58:22.799
They now could look at how the bombs were built.

00:58:24.000 --> 00:58:25.101
Inside this device,

00:58:25.568 --> 00:58:27.737
after it is tripped by opening it up,

00:58:28.371 --> 00:58:29.405
this would trip the device,

00:58:29.506 --> 00:58:29.973
not doing...

00:58:30.206 --> 00:58:46.222
the latch but opening it up like this would trip the device like that that would cause the detonator to explode inside here and hit this about four pounds of dynamite also included inside of this device

00:58:46.823 --> 00:58:54.364
was about 11 and a half pound of shrapnel and these shrapnel pieces were of 28 different varieties.

00:58:56.199 --> 00:58:57.500
Propelled by the dynamite,

00:58:58.001 --> 00:59:01.337
each piece of shrapnel travels ten times faster than a bullet.

00:59:02.472 --> 00:59:05.742
Moving at 14,000 to 15,000 feet per second,

00:59:06.242 --> 00:59:08.778
the shards can tear through solid concrete.

00:59:11.180 --> 00:59:13.316
Explosives are grouped in two categories,

00:59:13.983 --> 00:59:16.185
high explosives and low explosives.

00:59:17.620 --> 00:59:18.488
High explosives,

00:59:18.555 --> 00:59:19.656
like dynamite and C4,

00:59:20.390 --> 00:59:21.357
are the most powerful.

00:59:26.629 --> 00:59:31.301
They are used commercially for demolition and excavation and for military applications.

00:59:36.439 --> 00:59:37.907
Very stable to work with,

00:59:38.308 --> 00:59:40.810
high explosives require a great amount of force,

00:59:41.210 --> 00:59:41.911
a detonator,

00:59:42.245 --> 00:59:43.012
to set them off.

00:59:53.856 --> 01:00:00.229
Low explosives include the flash powder used in firecrackers and smokeless powder used in shotgun shells.

01:00:01.264 --> 01:00:06.569
They're easier to ignite than high explosives and are the explosive of choice for amateur bombers.

01:00:11.574 --> 01:00:15.178
By looking at the degree of destruction at the New York bomb scenes,

01:00:15.578 --> 01:00:19.582
investigators knew immediately that it was caused by high explosives.

01:00:24.153 --> 01:00:24.921
James Crippen,

01:00:25.655 --> 01:00:28.858
a nine-year veteran with the Colorado Bureau of Investigation,

01:00:29.258 --> 01:00:34.430
trains law enforcement agencies to conduct bomb investigations at the scene and in the lab.

01:00:36.099 --> 01:00:39.602
He's seen his share of the havoc wrought by high explosives.

01:00:40.603 --> 01:00:47.610
Certainly a pound of C4 is going to do a whole lot more damage than a pound of black powder.

01:00:47.877 --> 01:00:48.111
You know,

01:00:48.478 --> 01:00:49.879
a pound of black powder,

01:00:50.480 --> 01:00:50.747
you know,

01:00:51.981 --> 01:00:53.282
make a pretty good bomb.

01:00:53.449 --> 01:00:53.650
But,

01:00:53.683 --> 01:00:53.950
you know,

01:00:53.950 --> 01:00:55.485
a pound of C4 will,

01:00:55.852 --> 01:00:56.085
you know,

01:00:56.185 --> 01:00:57.387
can devastate a building.

01:00:58.287 --> 01:00:58.588
I mean,

01:00:58.688 --> 01:01:09.932
you're talking a difference in detonation velocity of anywhere from 1,200 to maybe 1,800 feet per second for black powder to in the neighborhood of 25,000 feet.

01:01:10.066 --> 01:01:12.068
per second for C4.

01:01:12.068 --> 01:01:12.835
So there's a lot,

01:01:13.436 --> 01:01:14.937
lot more force involved,

01:01:14.971 --> 01:01:16.572
a lot more shattering force.

01:01:19.475 --> 01:01:23.880
But sometimes people can survive a bomb blast even from a high explosive.

01:01:24.881 --> 01:01:29.118
Roosevelt Town resident William Lazor was a target of the New York serial bomber,

01:01:29.619 --> 01:01:31.521
but his suspicion saved his life.

01:01:32.722 --> 01:01:35.324
When Lazor picked up his package from the post office,

01:01:35.725 --> 01:01:37.794
the clerk joked that it might be a bomb.

01:01:41.664 --> 01:01:43.366
Though the clerk was trying to be funny,

01:01:43.966 --> 01:01:47.403
Lazor was careful enough to open the toolbox with his rake.

01:01:48.271 --> 01:01:53.476
That added bit of distance and caution saved his life when the bomb exploded.

01:01:57.580 --> 01:01:59.849
Several hundred miles away in Chictahuaga,

01:02:00.383 --> 01:02:02.318
Robert Fowler was less fortunate.

01:02:03.186 --> 01:02:06.222
Eleanor Fowler's husband received his package at work.

01:02:09.425 --> 01:02:14.564
These pictures were taken from a surveillance camera that captures a still image every few seconds.

01:02:15.598 --> 01:02:20.269
They count down the final moments in the lives of Robert Fowler and his co-worker,

01:02:20.536 --> 01:02:21.370
John O'Donnell.

01:02:22.638 --> 01:02:23.840
Just before the explosion,

01:02:24.173 --> 01:02:25.108
another co-worker,

01:02:25.341 --> 01:02:26.042
Jeffrey Kemp,

01:02:26.542 --> 01:02:28.911
offered to open the package with his pocket knife.

01:02:33.049 --> 01:02:34.584
The blast was so powerful,

01:02:34.884 --> 01:02:38.054
it threw Fowler some 15 feet down the hallway.

01:02:39.522 --> 01:02:40.189
Miraculously,

01:02:40.556 --> 01:02:44.227
Camp survived and can be seen fleeing the scene for help.

01:02:49.031 --> 01:02:49.799
Six bombs,

01:02:50.533 --> 01:02:51.634
four explosions,

01:02:52.435 --> 01:02:52.568
four

01:02:52.568 --> 01:02:54.070
Four counties in New York State.

01:02:56.372 --> 01:02:58.941
Each unexploded bomb was nearly identical,

01:02:59.509 --> 01:03:02.211
packaged in a toolbox and mailed from Buffalo.

01:03:03.446 --> 01:03:05.214
The bombing shared another element.

01:03:06.015 --> 01:03:08.451
All the intended victims were related.

01:03:08.851 --> 01:03:10.019
All the family members are interviewed.

01:03:10.019 --> 01:03:12.455
There were other family members who were not targeted,

01:03:13.723 --> 01:03:15.124
who were found and interviewed,

01:03:16.759 --> 01:03:17.927
and who survived.

01:03:18.761 --> 01:03:20.096
And everyone was asked,

01:03:20.096 --> 01:03:20.296
you know,

01:03:20.329 --> 01:03:21.397
who do you think might have done this?

01:03:21.430 --> 01:03:22.265
And everybody said,

01:03:22.298 --> 01:03:22.498
well,

01:03:23.299 --> 01:03:23.533
you know,

01:03:23.566 --> 01:03:24.200
Mike Stevens.

01:03:24.433 --> 01:03:25.034
Maybe he did it.

01:03:27.670 --> 01:03:31.240
56-year-old Mike Stevens had a girlfriend named Brenda Shavir.

01:03:32.241 --> 01:03:35.411
Everyone targeted in the bombing was related to Brenda.

01:03:36.779 --> 01:03:38.681
The connection might have been a coincidence,

01:03:39.015 --> 01:03:41.317
except for one more revealing detail.

01:03:42.418 --> 01:03:43.352
Three months earlier,

01:03:43.586 --> 01:03:52.128
the Monroe County Sheriff's bomb squad told Sammis that a restaurant manager near Rochester found more than 100 sticks of dynamite near a dumpster.

01:03:53.296 --> 01:03:57.900
The code numbers on the dynamite matched the dynamite in the unexploded bombs.

01:04:00.336 --> 01:04:03.439
Investigators traced the numbers to the Kentucky Powder Company.

01:04:04.473 --> 01:04:04.974
The firm,

01:04:05.308 --> 01:04:06.209
housed in a quarry,

01:04:06.609 --> 01:04:08.177
sold blasting supplies.

01:04:09.445 --> 01:04:17.186
Employees gave Samus a description of a man who had purchased 55 pounds of dynamite and 50 blasting caps four months earlier.

01:04:18.487 --> 01:04:18.888
Samus'

01:04:18.988 --> 01:04:21.958
investigation revealed this man to be Earl Figley,

01:04:22.525 --> 01:04:24.560
a close friend of Mike Stevens.

01:04:26.262 --> 01:04:27.563
Samus questioned Figley.

01:04:28.664 --> 01:04:30.766
Presented with all the evidence implicating him,

01:04:31.234 --> 01:04:36.038
Figley confessed that he and Stevens made the bombs and sent them to Brenda's family.

01:04:37.440 --> 01:04:39.008
Figley explained Stevens'

01:04:39.041 --> 01:04:39.642
motivation.

01:04:40.776 --> 01:04:45.248
Mike wanted to exact revenge on his girlfriend's family for not accepting him.

01:04:46.215 --> 01:04:48.951
The relationship between the two started becoming strained,

01:04:49.952 --> 01:04:50.386
and

01:04:51.821 --> 01:04:53.856
Mike started tapping her phone calls.

01:04:55.191 --> 01:04:57.126
And just as in any relationship,

01:04:57.860 --> 01:05:02.064
your own family is going to support you in your relationship with another person.

01:05:02.999 --> 01:05:04.433
And that's what happened in this case.

01:05:06.435 --> 01:05:07.436
Things weren't going well.

01:05:08.070 --> 01:05:10.172
Family members of Brenda would tell her,

01:05:11.641 --> 01:05:12.541
if it's not working out,

01:05:12.942 --> 01:05:14.343
why don't you get out of the relationship?

01:05:16.879 --> 01:05:21.117
Police had enough evidence to arrest both Earl Figley and Mike Stevens,

01:05:21.751 --> 01:05:25.521
but they still had to build a case that could not be beaten in court.

01:05:26.455 --> 01:05:26.889
For that,

01:05:27.290 --> 01:05:30.793
they needed hard evidence to convict Mike Stevens.

01:05:37.800 --> 01:05:39.969
In New York in 1994,

01:05:40.436 --> 01:05:45.775
detectives continued to follow every lead as they built their case against a serial bomber.

01:05:47.143 --> 01:05:49.812
While Mike Stevens and Earl Figley were in jail,

01:05:50.279 --> 01:05:54.116
the ATF National Response Team searched Figley's hotel room.

01:05:58.087 --> 01:06:02.124
They found boxes identical to the ones used to send the bombs.

01:06:07.363 --> 01:06:16.205
A tip led ATF agents and the Rochester Police Bomb Squad to a cache of evidence in a storage locker Stevens had rented several months earlier.

01:06:20.743 --> 01:06:21.610
Inside the locker,

01:06:21.877 --> 01:06:24.480
police uncovered dynamite and blasting caps.

01:06:25.781 --> 01:06:32.555
The explosives provided the ATF and other agencies with the ammunition they needed in their case against Stevens.

01:06:34.490 --> 01:06:37.660
But the incriminating evidence didn't stop with the explosives.

01:06:39.428 --> 01:06:44.900
They also recovered disguises Figley wore when he purchased equipment and mailed the bombs.

01:06:45.601 --> 01:06:46.035
A wig,

01:06:46.635 --> 01:06:47.003
beard,

01:06:47.436 --> 01:06:48.571
and theatrical makeup.

01:06:51.941 --> 01:06:57.546
They also found the books Stevens and Figley studied to learn how to construct the deadly weapons.

01:06:59.181 --> 01:07:04.320
The fact that these books exist represents the risk we take living in a free society,

01:07:04.820 --> 01:07:07.023
according to BOM expert James Crippen.

01:07:07.790 --> 01:07:10.926
The information has always been around on how to build devices.

01:07:11.193 --> 01:07:20.669
That's one of the things that we live with in the United States because we have a very rigid view of freedom of information,

01:07:20.669 --> 01:07:21.070
no matter.

01:07:22.738 --> 01:07:25.007
how much trouble it's going to cause us down the road.

01:07:25.241 --> 01:07:27.476
And I certainly agree with that point of view that,

01:07:27.510 --> 01:07:27.743
you know,

01:07:27.743 --> 01:07:29.178
you don't want to restrict information.

01:07:29.645 --> 01:07:32.148
You just hope that people will use it responsibly.

01:07:34.550 --> 01:07:37.420
Besides the explosives and books found in storage,

01:07:37.920 --> 01:07:40.856
investigators also found tools in Stephen's home.

01:07:41.991 --> 01:07:44.393
And like most everything else used in a crime,

01:07:45.227 --> 01:07:46.662
tools can be traced.

01:07:48.564 --> 01:07:50.699
At the ATF laboratory in Rockville,

01:07:50.699 --> 01:07:51.200
Maryland,

01:07:51.600 --> 01:07:55.838
forensic investigators carefully studied wire cutters they found in Stevens'

01:07:55.905 --> 01:07:56.205
home.

01:07:57.440 --> 01:08:04.046
Tool mark specialists had to match the wire cutters with the marks they made on the wires found in all the bombs.

01:08:05.548 --> 01:08:07.783
Tool mark examiner Elizabeth Gillis.

01:08:08.150 --> 01:08:11.587
When you have two tools that come off the production line one after the other,

01:08:11.654 --> 01:08:14.023
they're each going to have their own individuality,

01:08:14.423 --> 01:08:16.425
their own individual marks so that when you do,

01:08:16.926 --> 01:08:23.032
make a test cut with the tools you can put them on the microscope and be able to differentiate between which tool made which mark.

01:08:24.433 --> 01:08:24.834
First

01:08:25.201 --> 01:08:28.637
Gillis cuts a piece of wire using the cutter she wants to test.

01:08:29.505 --> 01:08:32.141
The wire is the same type found in the evidence.

01:08:33.142 --> 01:08:37.379
Investigators must make dozens of cuts with the tool in order to study the angle,

01:08:37.746 --> 01:08:38.581
contact area,

01:08:38.848 --> 01:08:40.316
and other attributes of the cut.

01:08:42.818 --> 01:08:46.388
Only then can they match a wire with the tool that cut it.

01:08:50.326 --> 01:08:52.561
She places the test cut under a microscope,

01:08:53.262 --> 01:08:58.968
orienting it to the appropriate angle so that she can compare the test cut with the wire found in evidence.

01:09:01.604 --> 01:09:06.308
She then superimposes the two images to see if the two cut patterns match.

01:09:13.249 --> 01:09:14.783
In the case of Mike Stevens,

01:09:15.050 --> 01:09:17.186
investigators made a successful match.

01:09:18.921 --> 01:09:24.894
The wires used in all the bombs had been cut by the wire cutters found in the basement of Stevens'

01:09:24.927 --> 01:09:25.294
house.

01:09:31.133 --> 01:09:31.800
While the books,

01:09:32.134 --> 01:09:32.635
wires,

01:09:32.735 --> 01:09:36.338
and tools helped investigators tighten their grip on Mike Stevens,

01:09:36.972 --> 01:09:39.441
they still wanted one more piece of evidence.

01:09:42.011 --> 01:09:46.081
They needed to establish a connection between the explosives and the suspect.

01:09:47.216 --> 01:09:49.618
People think that when a bomb goes off and blows up,

01:09:50.152 --> 01:09:51.253
that everything goes away.

01:09:51.820 --> 01:09:53.389
There's nothing further from the truth.

01:09:55.191 --> 01:09:56.659
When you have an explosion,

01:09:57.359 --> 01:10:03.966
all the explosive material is converted into a gaseous state that actually is consumed in the explosion.

01:10:04.767 --> 01:10:04.967
Well,

01:10:05.034 --> 01:10:06.569
that will go out as a heated gas,

01:10:06.602 --> 01:10:08.070
and when it hits other objects,

01:10:08.103 --> 01:10:11.607
it will cool down and start coating those objects,

01:10:11.607 --> 01:10:14.810
such as metal or clothing or wood or whatever.

01:10:14.877 --> 01:10:18.414
So not only will you have unreacted particles of explosives,

01:10:18.981 --> 01:10:22.851
but you'll have this gaseous film that's developed

01:10:23.052 --> 01:10:26.155
on other objects that you can test and get the residues from.

01:10:28.490 --> 01:10:30.359
At the ATF lab in Rockville,

01:10:30.426 --> 01:10:30.893
Maryland,

01:10:31.560 --> 01:10:37.032
Doug Klapek analyzed residue from the bomb scene to determine the kind of explosive used.

01:10:38.133 --> 01:10:38.968
Once identified,

01:10:39.368 --> 01:10:43.005
Klapek tried to match it with evidence collected from Mike Stevens.

01:10:44.907 --> 01:10:47.509
The residue from explosives evaporates quickly,

01:10:48.244 --> 01:10:51.513
so samples of the debris were packed in airtight canisters.

01:10:52.648 --> 01:10:54.283
To test for explosive residue,

01:10:54.717 --> 01:10:56.952
Klapek had to capture it as it dissipated.

01:10:58.053 --> 01:11:00.189
He prepared a charcoal-filled pipette,

01:11:00.623 --> 01:11:02.958
which he then inserted into the top of the canister.

01:11:08.397 --> 01:11:10.599
The apparatus was then attached to a vacuum.

01:11:14.270 --> 01:11:16.071
As the canister was gently heated,

01:11:16.739 --> 01:11:18.774
air was drawn out through the pipette.

01:11:20.175 --> 01:11:24.980
The charcoal inside the pipette grabbed the chemical components from the evidence.

01:11:27.983 --> 01:11:31.954
These chemicals were analyzed to see what explosive they came from.

01:11:36.425 --> 01:11:41.430
The test confirmed that dynamite was the explosive used in these bombs.

01:11:43.766 --> 01:11:44.099
Next,

01:11:44.433 --> 01:11:48.470
Clay Peck had to prove that Stevens and Figley had recently handled dynamite.

01:11:49.438 --> 01:11:50.139
If they had,

01:11:50.639 --> 01:11:53.075
its residue would have remained on their clothes.

01:11:53.642 --> 01:11:55.044
I found off of Stevens'

01:11:55.177 --> 01:11:57.579
boots and his clothes ethylene glycol dinitrate,

01:11:58.347 --> 01:12:00.149
which is only found in dynamite.

01:12:00.149 --> 01:12:03.452
There's no commercial use other than in dynamite for this material.

01:12:06.188 --> 01:12:08.057
Investigators had made their case.

01:12:08.857 --> 01:12:14.396
They had matched Stevens and Figley to explosives and the tools used to make the bombs.

01:12:16.598 --> 01:12:20.736
Any missing details were sewn together by Earl Figley himself.

01:12:23.539 --> 01:12:24.440
With this evidence,

01:12:25.007 --> 01:12:32.381
ATF agents determined that Mike Stevens constructed six bombs in the Rochester hotel room with the help of Earl Figley.

01:12:34.116 --> 01:12:34.550
In fact,

01:12:34.817 --> 01:12:36.251
to shift blame from himself,

01:12:37.019 --> 01:12:40.589
Stevens made sure Figley played a significant role in the murders.

01:12:41.657 --> 01:12:44.159
Mike Stevens was using Earl.

01:12:45.327 --> 01:12:47.563
to do all his dirty work for him.

01:12:48.130 --> 01:12:49.932
And he was successful,

01:12:49.965 --> 01:12:50.332
I guess,

01:12:50.399 --> 01:12:52.668
for a few hours anyway,

01:12:53.135 --> 01:12:54.236
but not for very long.

01:12:56.638 --> 01:12:58.640
Stephen's motive was vengeance.

01:12:59.274 --> 01:13:03.412
He was determined to make Brenda Shavir's family pay for rejecting him.

01:13:05.681 --> 01:13:07.449
To be certain the bombs were fatal,

01:13:08.884 --> 01:13:10.519
He loaded each with shrapnel.

01:13:11.019 --> 01:13:15.057
There is no doubt in my mind that these devices were designed to kill somebody upon opening.

01:13:15.090 --> 01:13:19.161
They're a strictly booby-trapped device addressed to a specific person.

01:13:22.064 --> 01:13:24.133
In order to make the bombs harder to trace,

01:13:24.533 --> 01:13:27.302
Stevens stuffed the packages with newspapers from Buffalo,

01:13:27.803 --> 01:13:30.139
so it would appear the packages were sent from there,

01:13:30.639 --> 01:13:32.307
not from his home in Rochester.

01:13:36.011 --> 01:13:36.445
In court,

01:13:36.979 --> 01:13:38.781
Figley implicated Mike Stevens,

01:13:39.181 --> 01:13:40.282
then pleaded guilty.

01:13:41.150 --> 01:13:43.452
He was sentenced to 20 years in prison.

01:13:46.355 --> 01:13:48.524
Convicted of the deaths of five people,

01:13:49.558 --> 01:13:52.861
Stevens was sentenced to four consecutive life terms.

01:13:56.498 --> 01:13:57.166
Most times,

01:13:57.399 --> 01:13:58.634
when a bomb detonates,

01:13:58.934 --> 01:14:01.103
it begins an agonizing catastrophe.

01:14:03.038 --> 01:14:04.640
But in a small town in Georgia,

01:14:05.240 --> 01:14:07.910
explosions are an everyday occurrence.

01:14:16.718 --> 01:14:17.453
Worldwide,

01:14:17.686 --> 01:14:20.322
the number of bombings is increasing every year.

01:14:21.290 --> 01:14:21.890
An alcohol,

01:14:21.957 --> 01:14:22.324
tobacco,

01:14:22.391 --> 01:14:26.528
and firearm study revealed that from 1990 to 1994,

01:14:26.995 --> 01:14:31.166
the number of reported bombings nearly doubled in the United States alone.

01:14:32.968 --> 01:14:33.669
Pete Mastin,

01:14:34.036 --> 01:14:35.304
head of ATF training,

01:14:35.737 --> 01:14:38.707
has investigated explosions for more than 30 years.

01:14:39.508 --> 01:14:39.641
Well,

01:14:39.641 --> 01:14:44.112
the typical bomber is perhaps the ultimate coward.

01:14:46.615 --> 01:14:49.685
The typical bomber doesn't face,

01:14:51.153 --> 01:14:52.888
nor do they want to face,

01:14:53.355 --> 01:14:54.056
their opponent.

01:14:54.156 --> 01:15:00.996
They would much prefer to use whatever subterfuge and cover they can.

01:15:03.732 --> 01:15:06.335
To make sure these criminals are brought to justice,

01:15:06.702 --> 01:15:11.607
the ATF brings young investigators and students to this testing ground in Brunswick,

01:15:11.673 --> 01:15:12.007
Georgia,

01:15:12.474 --> 01:15:14.409
to teach them how to catch a bomber.

01:15:17.980 --> 01:15:18.280
Here,

01:15:18.580 --> 01:15:20.916
the ATF trains evidence technicians,

01:15:21.283 --> 01:15:22.284
fire investigators,

01:15:22.618 --> 01:15:23.619
schematic artists,

01:15:23.785 --> 01:15:25.287
and explosives technicians.

01:15:27.222 --> 01:15:27.923
At the academy,

01:15:28.390 --> 01:15:30.826
each skill is refined by repetition.

01:15:32.461 --> 01:15:33.061
On this day,

01:15:33.629 --> 01:15:36.598
ATF bomb scene instructors will test their students'

01:15:36.598 --> 01:15:39.134
knowledge by detonating three car bombs,

01:15:39.635 --> 01:15:41.503
simulating real crime scenes.

01:15:44.506 --> 01:15:49.011
The men building and packaging these explosives are highly trained experts.

01:15:50.345 --> 01:15:53.515
Their job is as delicate as it is dangerous.

01:16:07.763 --> 01:16:11.500
One cannot underestimate the power of the explosive materials.

01:16:12.701 --> 01:16:13.235
That's why,

01:16:13.402 --> 01:16:14.937
in inexperienced hands,

01:16:15.370 --> 01:16:18.373
bomb-making information can be a recipe for disaster,

01:16:18.807 --> 01:16:21.109
as instructor Larry Casey explains.

01:16:21.810 --> 01:16:26.348
All that information that's available doesn't have proper safety precautions in it,

01:16:26.882 --> 01:16:27.449
and the learnings...

01:16:27.583 --> 01:16:31.386
skills of sequential assembly processes,

01:16:31.453 --> 01:16:33.522
things that are important to know for safety reasons,

01:16:33.989 --> 01:16:35.023
are totally missing there.

01:16:35.090 --> 01:16:37.793
So they're always at great risk to blow themselves up.

01:16:38.393 --> 01:16:52.574
And we often times will get a call and you'll find the tragic aftermath in a garage or someplace where the kids were doing experimentation and what you get are pieces of bodies and tools scattered around and death and destruction and that's the sad part of this.

01:16:56.311 --> 01:17:00.582
Instructors at the Academy begin the training session by building pipe bombs.

01:17:03.018 --> 01:17:04.820
These bombs are not mock-ups.

01:17:05.153 --> 01:17:05.854
They are real.

01:17:06.888 --> 01:17:09.925
The explosive powder must be filled with great care.

01:17:10.759 --> 01:17:14.429
Just a few grains stuck in the pipe thread could trigger a blast.

01:17:15.964 --> 01:17:16.865
A small spark,

01:17:17.199 --> 01:17:18.567
or even static electricity,

01:17:19.034 --> 01:17:20.068
could ignite the powder.

01:17:26.742 --> 01:17:27.376
For the test,

01:17:27.643 --> 01:17:32.381
the bomb is placed so the end caps shoot into the engine compartment and the back seat,

01:17:33.081 --> 01:17:36.485
making the work of finding them more of a challenge for the students.

01:17:36.952 --> 01:17:41.289
So that big high-pressure end cap should go right into that radio area.

01:17:41.356 --> 01:17:44.259
So it's fore and aft kind of at this angle?

01:17:44.259 --> 01:17:44.459
Yeah.

01:17:44.626 --> 01:17:44.893
Okay.

01:17:46.161 --> 01:17:47.829
When everyone has cleared the area,

01:17:48.296 --> 01:17:49.698
it's time for detonation.

01:18:01.376 --> 01:18:02.310
After the explosion,

01:18:02.811 --> 01:18:05.313
ATF investigators inspect the wreckage.

01:18:22.664 --> 01:18:26.401
Some of the lighter fragments were propelled hundreds of feet into the woods,

01:18:27.002 --> 01:18:29.237
creating a huge area to investigate.

01:18:32.040 --> 01:18:39.114
These fragments provide the students with valuable clues about the makeup of the bomb and where it was placed inside the car.

01:18:41.116 --> 01:18:41.717
In the field,

01:18:42.184 --> 01:18:43.518
nothing can be overlooked.

01:18:44.386 --> 01:18:47.656
Investigators have only one chance to gather evidence.

01:18:48.290 --> 01:18:49.591
Once the scene is released,

01:18:50.358 --> 01:18:51.693
it's going to be very difficult,

01:18:51.893 --> 01:18:53.662
if not impossible in some cases,

01:18:53.695 --> 01:18:57.966
to come back into that scene or to recreate that scene hours,

01:18:58.100 --> 01:18:58.433
days,

01:18:58.467 --> 01:18:59.234
or weeks later.

01:18:59.601 --> 01:19:02.003
So it's a one-shot opportunity for perfection.

01:19:12.514 --> 01:19:16.618
It quickly becomes evident how destructive these small pipe bombs can be.

01:19:18.120 --> 01:19:23.825
This explosion was so powerful that part of the cardboard carton pierced the car's steel door.

01:19:27.329 --> 01:19:30.599
Other fragments sliced through the roof as if it were foil.

01:19:35.837 --> 01:19:41.977
Instructors prepare the next bomb by mixing several low explosives into one powerful cocktail.

01:19:53.021 --> 01:19:54.589
The work is nerve-wracking.

01:19:57.759 --> 01:20:03.064
The powder must be tamped into the pipe and the detonator delicately taped to the loaded bomb.

01:20:20.682 --> 01:20:20.782
In

01:20:20.849 --> 01:20:21.416
some cases,

01:20:21.750 --> 01:20:27.622
the seat cushions are wetted down to prevent fire from destroying evidence the students will need to work with.

01:20:31.626 --> 01:20:34.095
The final package is ready to detonate.

01:20:37.465 --> 01:20:49.711
We just got...

01:21:08.129 --> 01:21:09.231
After the explosions,

01:21:09.497 --> 01:21:11.333
the students divide into teams.

01:21:12.167 --> 01:21:14.836
The team leader assigns each person to a task.

01:21:16.171 --> 01:21:16.605
Charlie,

01:21:16.638 --> 01:21:16.872
Ray,

01:21:17.305 --> 01:21:17.505
Rick,

01:21:17.839 --> 01:21:18.173
Frank,

01:21:18.340 --> 01:21:18.673
Joe.

01:21:19.007 --> 01:21:22.010
And Colin's going to kind of be the overseer of that group.

01:21:22.344 --> 01:21:22.978
What you found...

01:21:23.011 --> 01:21:27.349
If you feel like you found something that's a really critical piece of evidence that's going to give us,

01:21:27.949 --> 01:21:29.484
particularly the guys that are on the car,

01:21:29.851 --> 01:21:35.390
to give them an idea on what the device is to help them look more deeply into certain things,

01:21:35.490 --> 01:21:36.324
let's bring that up.

01:21:38.627 --> 01:21:41.596
The students work together to investigate the bomb scene.

01:21:42.597 --> 01:21:43.298
In the field,

01:21:43.632 --> 01:21:47.502
ATF agents have to work quickly and under a great deal of pressure.

01:21:48.637 --> 01:21:50.205
Students are taught early on.

01:21:50.505 --> 01:21:53.041
They will have to work with state and local authorities,

01:21:53.475 --> 01:21:54.643
laboratory technicians,

01:21:54.976 --> 01:21:55.977
and most importantly,

01:21:56.278 --> 01:21:56.711
each other,

01:21:57.112 --> 01:21:58.213
to solve a crime.

01:22:01.983 --> 01:22:05.553
One team begins its work by forming a perimeter around the scene.

01:22:06.688 --> 01:22:11.359
Then they divide the area into quadrants before they begin their search for fragments.

01:22:23.505 --> 01:22:23.605
Hey,

01:22:24.172 --> 01:22:24.339
Jim.

01:22:24.372 --> 01:22:24.773
Just look at him.

01:22:24.773 --> 01:22:24.940
Colin.

01:22:24.940 --> 01:22:32.714
He just looks like a piece of the ANCAP,

01:22:32.781 --> 01:22:33.214
right?

01:22:33.381 --> 01:22:35.150
The students walk along the tarmac,

01:22:35.617 --> 01:22:39.454
advancing as a team so they don't miss any shard of evidence.

01:22:42.724 --> 01:22:48.396
Each piece is then identified with a chalk circle or a small orange cone and assigned a number.

01:22:49.864 --> 01:22:51.533
In an explosive scene like this,

01:22:52.033 --> 01:22:54.469
evidence numbers can reach into the thousands.

01:23:00.208 --> 01:23:06.014
One team member records the identification number and exact location of each piece of evidence.

01:23:13.488 --> 01:23:17.258
Distances between the fragments are measured to plot their location.

01:23:22.964 --> 01:23:26.267
Another team closely inspects the damage inside the car.

01:23:27.302 --> 01:23:32.607
Their job is to figure out which fragments are part of the car and which may be parts of the bomb.

01:23:35.377 --> 01:23:36.378
For many of the students,

01:23:36.745 --> 01:23:40.715
this is the first time they have seen a bomb's devastation up close.

01:23:41.750 --> 01:23:43.184
it won't be their last.

01:23:45.120 --> 01:23:47.589
And while the damage to the car looks complete,

01:23:48.256 --> 01:23:51.159
valuable evidence remains embedded in the wreckage.

01:23:53.361 --> 01:23:53.828
Hatchets,

01:23:54.129 --> 01:23:54.596
hammers,

01:23:54.662 --> 01:23:58.366
and crowbars are brought in to pull these clues from the vehicle.

01:24:00.368 --> 01:24:07.175
The car is literally dissected as students search for pieces of the bomb lodged in or behind the dashboard.

01:24:08.777 --> 01:24:12.947
They never know where they might find the most significant piece of evidence.

01:24:15.450 --> 01:24:18.319
The students then move the car and search underneath.

01:24:20.989 --> 01:24:23.058
Bombs explode in all directions,

01:24:23.458 --> 01:24:25.960
and clues typically become embedded in the asphalt.

01:24:36.204 --> 01:24:39.074
Inspectors then sweep up what remains of the scene.

01:24:44.379 --> 01:24:51.586
The debris is then carefully sifted through a thin wire screen to separate any small clues that may have been overlooked.

01:25:04.899 --> 01:25:11.506
Instructors intensify the pressure and pump up the realism by adding a chaotic element to the bomb scene.

01:25:12.373 --> 01:25:14.008
They're called survivors.

01:25:20.748 --> 01:25:27.122
Role players portray panicked victims and family members whose loved ones have been killed or injured in the blast.

01:25:28.189 --> 01:25:30.825
The scenarios are built on actual occurrences.

01:25:31.893 --> 01:25:37.031
The students must learn to be firm but compassionate in the emotionally charged atmosphere.

01:25:39.033 --> 01:25:41.603
As the actors rush toward the bombed out car,

01:25:42.203 --> 01:25:47.475
they're stopped by students who must be able to comfort them and keep them clear of the investigation.

01:25:51.112 --> 01:25:52.280
Once the scene is cleared,

01:25:52.547 --> 01:25:54.282
it is photographed and videotaped.

01:25:55.383 --> 01:25:58.086
Fragments are then collected and sent to a lab.

01:26:01.789 --> 01:26:07.729
These thorough investigation methods must be learned if the students ever hope to catch a bomber.

01:26:08.196 --> 01:26:13.001
You've got to be able to prove your investigation in a court of law.

01:26:13.635 --> 01:26:15.136
And most of the time,

01:26:15.203 --> 01:26:22.977
that takes forensic evidence that puts the bomb in the hands of the bomber.

01:26:25.580 --> 01:26:26.414
In the classroom,

01:26:26.748 --> 01:26:28.116
they get an anatomy lesson,

01:26:28.950 --> 01:26:31.119
learning the vital parts of a bomb.

01:26:33.054 --> 01:26:33.321
Tape,

01:26:33.855 --> 01:26:34.122
pipe,

01:26:34.689 --> 01:26:35.223
detonator,

01:26:35.490 --> 01:26:43.364
and wires are all carefully examined as the students begin to piece together the evidence gathered outside at the bombing scene.

01:26:53.007 --> 01:26:57.979
The need for well-trained ATF investigators is crucial for crime solving.

01:26:59.514 --> 01:26:59.948
Usually,

01:27:00.248 --> 01:27:01.416
when a bomb goes off,

01:27:01.749 --> 01:27:03.751
the bomber is miles away.

01:27:04.752 --> 01:27:05.987
But in a case in Florida,

01:27:06.454 --> 01:27:08.890
he struck frighteningly close to home.

01:27:17.298 --> 01:27:17.865
On July 9,

01:27:17.865 --> 01:27:19.601
1985,

01:27:20.001 --> 01:27:21.603
two deafening explosions,

01:27:21.903 --> 01:27:22.604
seconds apart,

01:27:23.137 --> 01:27:25.974
rocked a quiet suburban neighborhood in Naples,

01:27:26.040 --> 01:27:26.474
Florida.

01:27:30.578 --> 01:27:34.148
The Collier County Sheriff's deputies immediately arrived at the scene,

01:27:35.750 --> 01:27:39.387
but the explosion had already taken the lives of two people.

01:27:41.322 --> 01:27:44.425
Matron of the wealthy Benson and Hedges tobacco family,

01:27:44.759 --> 01:27:46.995
Margaret Benson and her adopted son,

01:27:47.095 --> 01:27:47.462
Scott,

01:27:47.729 --> 01:27:48.396
were killed.

01:27:49.397 --> 01:27:50.198
Margaret's daughter,

01:27:50.498 --> 01:27:51.132
Carol Lynn,

01:27:51.633 --> 01:27:52.700
was critically injured.

01:28:02.410 --> 01:28:06.347
The Sheriff's Office knew this type of devastation was no accident.

01:28:07.348 --> 01:28:09.217
They called the ATF for help.

01:28:10.585 --> 01:28:13.554
Special Agent Ralph Ostrowski worked the investigation.

01:28:14.022 --> 01:28:16.024
In my 22 years with ATF,

01:28:16.391 --> 01:28:17.992
particularly my time in Cleveland,

01:28:18.526 --> 01:28:18.793
when

01:28:19.327 --> 01:28:21.296
I worked a lot of organized crime,

01:28:22.063 --> 01:28:22.630
bombings,

01:28:22.930 --> 01:28:23.865
vehicle bombings,

01:28:24.299 --> 01:28:24.899
this was,

01:28:25.099 --> 01:28:25.900
without a doubt,

01:28:26.134 --> 01:28:27.969
the worst vehicle bombing

01:28:28.469 --> 01:28:29.270
I had ever seen.

01:28:36.778 --> 01:28:41.015
Ostrowski and his team immediately set out to discover what caused the destruction.

01:28:43.384 --> 01:28:47.989
Debris from the exploded Chevy Suburban was spread for yards around.

01:28:49.824 --> 01:28:50.558
Oftentimes,

01:28:51.392 --> 01:28:53.628
especially in a bombing of this magnitude,

01:28:54.028 --> 01:29:00.134
debris can be spread hundreds of feet well beyond what you think it would be.

01:29:02.704 --> 01:29:07.709
ATF agents worked with police to establish a procedure to conduct the evidence gathering.

01:29:09.777 --> 01:29:12.180
The agents had done this many times before,

01:29:12.680 --> 01:29:16.284
and they knew this case was going to take a lot of time to solve.

01:29:21.622 --> 01:29:23.291
So by the second or third day,

01:29:24.258 --> 01:29:25.059
needless to say,

01:29:25.059 --> 01:29:26.294
the scene got to be very,

01:29:26.527 --> 01:29:27.795
very tedious to work.

01:29:28.296 --> 01:29:33.801
The agents were forced to wear masks because of the stench,

01:29:34.469 --> 01:29:41.075
and they did an excellent job in recovering as much of the evidence as they did recover.

01:29:43.711 --> 01:29:45.246
Every fragment was marked.

01:29:47.415 --> 01:29:51.252
Chalk outlined where pieces of debris landed after the explosion.

01:30:01.195 --> 01:30:04.732
It didn't take long to discover the probable cause of the explosion.

01:30:07.235 --> 01:30:11.706
They found pieces of large metal pipes that weren't automobile parts.

01:30:16.577 --> 01:30:20.982
The ATF was confident the vehicle was destroyed by a powerful pipe bomb,

01:30:21.716 --> 01:30:25.620
but they now had to determine how it was constructed and who planted it.

01:30:26.320 --> 01:30:28.723
The vehicle was absolutely destroyed.

01:30:29.123 --> 01:30:31.459
This was a large vehicle in 1978.

01:30:31.659 --> 01:30:32.860
The Chevy Suburban,

01:30:32.960 --> 01:30:35.630
the top of it was peeled back.

01:30:37.331 --> 01:30:38.032
With two

01:30:38.866 --> 01:30:42.637
27-pound pipe bombs in a contained vehicle,

01:30:43.371 --> 01:30:47.508
the force of the explosion did a great deal of damage.

01:30:54.148 --> 01:30:56.217
As the evidence continued to accumulate,

01:30:56.717 --> 01:30:58.719
agents began questioning witnesses.

01:31:00.755 --> 01:31:01.055
Like I said,

01:31:01.055 --> 01:31:01.989
I went ahead and...

01:31:02.423 --> 01:31:05.092
The first person they spoke with was Steven Benson,

01:31:05.560 --> 01:31:07.962
the only family member to escape injury.

01:31:10.231 --> 01:31:12.333
His mother and his brother had been killed.

01:31:13.167 --> 01:31:15.503
His sister was fighting for her life in a hospital,

01:31:16.103 --> 01:31:19.841
yet police reported that Steven Benson's demeanor was perfectly calm,

01:31:20.475 --> 01:31:21.175
even upbeat.

01:31:22.710 --> 01:31:26.447
This raised the suspicions of Collier County Captain Tom Storer.

01:31:27.281 --> 01:31:34.155
My suspicions were aroused immediately that something is not right in what was going on.

01:31:34.455 --> 01:31:38.326
It just wasn't consistent with the catastrophic event that had just occurred.

01:31:41.229 --> 01:31:43.364
Within 24 hours of the explosion,

01:31:43.865 --> 01:31:46.801
Steven Benson stopped talking directly with police.

01:31:47.802 --> 01:31:49.937
All questions filtered through his attorney.

01:31:52.406 --> 01:31:54.275
Though suspicious about Steven Benson,

01:31:54.742 --> 01:31:57.912
investigators had to be careful not to jump to conclusions.

01:31:59.046 --> 01:31:59.547
So far,

01:31:59.647 --> 01:32:03.618
they had no solid evidence for or against Benson's involvement.

01:32:04.719 --> 01:32:07.622
It was too early in the game to launch accusations,

01:32:08.089 --> 01:32:11.292
and they had no time to waste following a false trail.

01:32:14.462 --> 01:32:17.798
We follow the evidence wherever that evidence is going to take us.

01:32:18.099 --> 01:32:23.938
If we focus on one subject and use all of our resources on that one subject...

01:32:24.705 --> 01:32:26.307
and we happen to be wrong,

01:32:26.641 --> 01:32:36.651
then we've wasted very valuable time early on in the investigation when it's probably one of the most critical periods.

01:32:39.287 --> 01:32:40.521
Back at the sheriff's office,

01:32:40.888 --> 01:32:44.025
investigators began to study all the evidence from the scene,

01:32:44.592 --> 01:32:47.595
including pieces of electronic circuitry and batteries,

01:32:48.729 --> 01:32:50.131
elements of a trigger device.

01:32:53.501 --> 01:32:58.472
They knew that whoever planted the bomb had some expertise in assembling electronics.

01:33:01.342 --> 01:33:06.948
Ralph Ostrowski and Jack Gant began to study a schematic artist's map of the explosion,

01:33:07.815 --> 01:33:11.819
giving them a clear look at the epicenter and direction of the blast.

01:33:12.386 --> 01:33:16.223
The purpose of these sketches that we're looking at here today is to assist the investigator when he...

01:33:16.223 --> 01:33:16.991
testifies in court,

01:33:17.525 --> 01:33:26.734
he'll be asked by the defense where each piece of these articles were picked up and he must be able to testify that he picked these articles up in a location around the car.

01:33:27.268 --> 01:33:29.904
He must be able to provide testimony of what this article is.

01:33:30.371 --> 01:33:36.444
He must be able to tell the date and time he got it and identify it later that this is the item that he picked up the particular day of the crime.

01:33:37.011 --> 01:33:37.912
Without that testimony,

01:33:37.979 --> 01:33:39.480
this evidence cannot be used in court.

01:33:41.782 --> 01:33:46.220
The schematic shows where every speck of evidence was found after the blast.

01:33:47.154 --> 01:33:55.262
Their small size and great distance from the car are testament to the tremendous power of the explosion as it tore through the vehicle.

01:33:59.700 --> 01:34:02.403
Once the maps were drawn and the evidence was collected,

01:34:02.770 --> 01:34:04.438
the investigation moved north.

01:34:05.606 --> 01:34:09.744
Fragments gathered at the scene were taken to the ATF laboratory in Atlanta,

01:34:09.844 --> 01:34:10.211
Georgia.

01:34:11.278 --> 01:34:11.545
Here,

01:34:11.646 --> 01:34:15.449
investigators would try to make sense of the twisted wreckage of the vehicle.

01:34:17.518 --> 01:34:19.186
Forensic chemist Walter Mitchell,

01:34:19.487 --> 01:34:19.587
a

01:34:20.254 --> 01:34:21.589
26-year veteran of the ATF,

01:34:21.989 --> 01:34:24.492
started with a visual inspection of the fragments.

01:34:26.060 --> 01:34:26.994
Through the microscope,

01:34:27.361 --> 01:34:31.399
Mitchell studied the small wafer-like specks clinging to the sample.

01:34:32.400 --> 01:34:35.736
He tentatively identified the particles as smokeless powder.

01:34:38.239 --> 01:34:40.608
To confirm he was dealing with smokeless powder,

01:34:41.075 --> 01:34:45.913
Mitchell had to test it chemically using a process called thin-layer chromatography.

01:34:50.017 --> 01:34:50.384
First,

01:34:50.685 --> 01:34:53.287
he coated a sheet of glass with an absorbent layer.

01:34:56.090 --> 01:35:00.628
Then he placed dots of the smokeless powder and his unknown sample on it.

01:35:05.566 --> 01:35:07.802
The glass was dipped into a tank of solvent.

01:35:08.903 --> 01:35:11.205
As the solvent was drawn up the absorbent layer,

01:35:11.672 --> 01:35:14.809
it carried with it small quantities of the dissolved powders.

01:35:16.077 --> 01:35:17.445
If the powders were the same,

01:35:17.912 --> 01:35:20.414
they'd migrate the same distance up the glass,

01:35:20.948 --> 01:35:23.551
forming streaks of identical length and color.

01:35:25.986 --> 01:35:27.521
Mitchell removed and dried it.

01:35:28.622 --> 01:35:33.160
Then he put the glass under ultraviolet light to compare the colors of the streaks.

01:35:37.231 --> 01:35:37.865
In this case,

01:35:38.165 --> 01:35:42.169
the color of the smokeless powder and the unknown sample matched.

01:35:43.337 --> 01:35:45.840
A comparison of the lengths of the streaks matched,

01:35:45.940 --> 01:35:46.173
too.

01:35:47.007 --> 01:35:49.376
The test confirmed Mitchell's suspicions.

01:35:50.144 --> 01:35:53.914
Smokeless powder was used to blow up the vehicle in Naples,

01:35:53.981 --> 01:35:54.415
Florida.

01:35:56.751 --> 01:36:02.623
But scientists in the laboratory could not find any way to link the bomber to the bomb fragments.

01:36:03.657 --> 01:36:06.727
That evidence would have to come from somewhere else.

01:36:12.633 --> 01:36:13.200
In Naples,

01:36:13.267 --> 01:36:13.701
Florida,

01:36:14.034 --> 01:36:21.308
forensic investigators began to track down the origin of the metal pipe they believed housed the bombs that killed the Bensons.

01:36:22.877 --> 01:36:26.247
Pipe manufacturers stamp a maker's mark on their products.

01:36:27.081 --> 01:36:27.648
Fortunately,

01:36:28.048 --> 01:36:31.352
enough of the logo survived the blast to be identified.

01:36:32.453 --> 01:36:36.757
Agents traced the pipes to the only company in town that sold that brand.

01:36:38.559 --> 01:36:41.896
A store employee told investigators that a few days earlier,

01:36:42.129 --> 01:36:46.200
he had sold the pipes to a man wearing a baseball cap and sunglasses.

01:36:47.201 --> 01:36:50.404
The buyer said he worked for the Delray Construction Company.

01:36:51.939 --> 01:36:55.276
Investigators later found out that no such company existed.

01:36:58.245 --> 01:37:00.247
They took the invoices as evidence.

01:37:01.048 --> 01:37:03.317
The signatures were nothing more than a scribble,

01:37:04.285 --> 01:37:06.687
but the paper contained something more revealing.

01:37:10.858 --> 01:37:13.761
The invoices bore the impressions of a palm print.

01:37:15.796 --> 01:37:17.698
Could they be the link to the suspect,

01:37:17.932 --> 01:37:18.699
Stephen Benson?

01:37:19.967 --> 01:37:23.237
Authorities obtained Benson's own palm prints for comparison.

01:37:24.305 --> 01:37:28.742
They hoped to find enough points of identification between the two to make a match.

01:37:29.610 --> 01:37:30.177
In this case,

01:37:30.211 --> 01:37:33.314
these palm prints that did come up on these forms are in excellent condition,

01:37:33.948 --> 01:37:37.818
and the points of identification probably reached near 60 to 75 points of ID.

01:37:38.519 --> 01:37:42.356
There's no question in anybody's mind that these palm prints are those of Steven Benson.

01:37:45.226 --> 01:37:50.097
A background check on Steven Benson uncovered his fascination with explosives.

01:37:52.867 --> 01:37:54.101
In a high school yearbook,

01:37:54.368 --> 01:37:57.004
a dedication he wrote to an old girlfriend read,

01:37:58.405 --> 01:37:59.840
I hope to see you this summer,

01:38:00.975 --> 01:38:02.676
then we can bomb around.

01:38:04.712 --> 01:38:14.722
We had developed information that he started experimenting with explosives back many years ago in Pennsylvania.

01:38:14.855 --> 01:38:19.760
We had talked to witnesses that saw him with firecracker

01:38:20.094 --> 01:38:25.666
M80, M100 type of devices using copper tubing,

01:38:26.367 --> 01:38:31.105
using remote control to set off some of these firecrackers.

01:38:41.081 --> 01:38:44.018
To understand Benson's possible motivation for murder,

01:38:44.952 --> 01:38:46.954
authorities looked to a financial auditor.

01:38:48.989 --> 01:38:50.557
The auditor determined that Benson,

01:38:50.925 --> 01:38:53.093
despite his access to a family fortune,

01:38:53.527 --> 01:38:55.029
was a terrible businessman.

01:38:57.398 --> 01:39:04.538
Police discovered that his financial failures were so far-ranging that Stephen started forging checks from his mother's accounts.

01:39:05.739 --> 01:39:07.541
When Margaret Benson discovered this,

01:39:08.042 --> 01:39:10.311
she threatened to write Stephen out of her will.

01:39:13.113 --> 01:39:17.751
Could it be Stephen Benson saw one clear way to get at those millions?

01:39:20.554 --> 01:39:29.596
Investigators believe Benson used his expertise in electronics to construct the two deadly bombs and the trigger device he used to set them off.

01:39:33.167 --> 01:39:34.301
On the morning of the bombing,

01:39:34.468 --> 01:39:37.738
Stephen told everyone he was going to pick up coffee and donuts.

01:39:39.707 --> 01:39:43.110
But police believe this is when he planted the bombs in the car.

01:39:45.312 --> 01:39:46.146
Once that was done,

01:39:46.647 --> 01:39:48.682
all he had to do was trigger the bombs,

01:39:49.083 --> 01:39:51.251
and a family fortune would be his.

01:39:52.720 --> 01:39:53.220
Or at least,

01:39:53.520 --> 01:39:54.655
that's what he planned.

01:40:00.494 --> 01:40:02.830
After Stephen placed the bombs under the car,

01:40:03.263 --> 01:40:04.331
he drove back home.

01:40:06.400 --> 01:40:08.936
He was gone approximately 45 minutes.

01:40:15.943 --> 01:40:16.577
Carol Lynn,

01:40:17.077 --> 01:40:17.444
Margaret,

01:40:17.745 --> 01:40:18.045
Scott,

01:40:18.245 --> 01:40:23.384
and Stephen set out to drive to the Gulf Coast to survey property for a new house.

01:40:26.420 --> 01:40:30.491
Carol Lynn reported that Stephen calmly walked side by side with the group,

01:40:31.125 --> 01:40:35.262
giving no indication of the tragedy he knew he was about to ignite.

01:40:38.065 --> 01:40:40.200
But as they were all about to get into the car,

01:40:41.301 --> 01:40:43.670
Carol Lynn noticed something was wrong.

01:40:45.072 --> 01:40:49.309
Caroline was able to tell us that the morning they got into the vehicle to leave,

01:40:49.943 --> 01:40:52.146
that things didn't look right at that point.

01:40:52.379 --> 01:40:54.114
She noticed that Scott was the driver.

01:40:54.281 --> 01:40:56.283
That was very rare that Scott was ever the driver.

01:40:56.683 --> 01:40:58.352
She noticed that Mother was in the front seat.

01:40:58.886 --> 01:41:00.487
Mother was seldom in the front seat,

01:41:00.487 --> 01:41:02.856
and that was her place because she got car sick a lot.

01:41:05.692 --> 01:41:06.760
According to his plan,

01:41:07.261 --> 01:41:09.830
Stephen made sure everyone got into the car.

01:41:16.103 --> 01:41:17.805
Just before they were about to leave,

01:41:18.205 --> 01:41:21.275
Stephen excused himself to pick up something he'd forgotten.

01:41:32.586 --> 01:41:34.721
He disappeared inside the house.

01:41:45.399 --> 01:41:51.371
The investigation into the bombings uncovered the evidence needed to arrest and convict Steven Benson.

01:41:52.906 --> 01:41:54.908
He was sentenced to life in prison.

01:41:58.879 --> 01:42:04.585
Bombs are built from common materials by people with an uncommon thirst for vengeance.

01:42:05.819 --> 01:42:06.820
Easily concealed,

01:42:07.421 --> 01:42:08.455
utterly devastating,

01:42:08.989 --> 01:42:11.125
a bomb can detonate any time,

01:42:11.658 --> 01:42:12.226
anywhere.

01:42:13.594 --> 01:42:20.334
It's this simple truth that makes bombing so challenging to investigators and so chilling for the rest of us.

01:42:21.802 --> 01:42:23.871
But investigators have the advantage.

01:42:24.738 --> 01:42:26.173
When a bomber does strike,

01:42:26.607 --> 01:42:29.843
he always leaves his weapon at the scene of the crime.

01:42:31.378 --> 01:42:34.414
And with it is the residue of his guilt.

01:42:37.718 --> 01:42:39.586
In 1963,

01:42:39.586 --> 01:42:42.789
detectives investigated the murder of a woman in California.

01:42:44.091 --> 01:42:48.262
But it would take technology almost 30 years to catch up with the killer.

01:42:53.901 --> 01:42:58.572
A trash bag is the only clue detectives have to the identity of a serial killer.

01:42:59.673 --> 01:43:00.207
So far,

01:43:00.274 --> 01:43:02.276
their search has met only with failure.

01:43:03.377 --> 01:43:08.482
But a new fingerprint technology will give them one last chance to put the killer away.

01:43:10.484 --> 01:43:12.186
In a murder investigation in Vermont,

01:43:12.553 --> 01:43:13.921
police have their suspect.

01:43:14.788 --> 01:43:17.824
Now all they need is solid evidence to convict him.

01:43:18.959 --> 01:43:23.197
The case hinges on a bloody but distorted palm print on the murder weapon.

01:43:26.466 --> 01:43:28.502
In cases that look all but hopeless,

01:43:29.102 --> 01:43:35.008
science finds a solution in the telltale marks of the killer's death grip.

01:44:00.701 --> 01:44:02.202
In 1985,

01:44:02.402 --> 01:44:05.105
a serial killer was on the loose in San Diego,

01:44:05.239 --> 01:44:05.906
California.

01:44:07.007 --> 01:44:09.409
The killer targeted prostitutes and other women.

01:44:10.310 --> 01:44:11.511
He'd rape and murder them,

01:44:12.012 --> 01:44:14.481
then discard their bodies in trash dumpsters.

01:44:15.649 --> 01:44:16.183
To catch him,

01:44:16.550 --> 01:44:18.819
police needed to identify his fingerprints,

01:44:19.386 --> 01:44:20.921
which proved to be elusive.

01:44:26.960 --> 01:44:27.894
On the morning of May 9th,

01:44:27.894 --> 01:44:29.496
1986,

01:44:29.830 --> 01:44:35.369
police responded to a call from a woman who came upon a ghastly sight as she was taking out her garbage,

01:44:36.036 --> 01:44:37.471
the killer's latest victim.

01:44:40.140 --> 01:44:40.974
When they arrived,

01:44:41.308 --> 01:44:43.810
they saw a sight that had become all too familiar,

01:44:44.444 --> 01:44:45.779
a body in a dumpster.

01:44:46.947 --> 01:44:47.447
This time,

01:44:47.714 --> 01:44:51.551
the murderer had wrapped it in two garbage bags joined with masking tape.

01:44:52.519 --> 01:44:54.288
After disposing of the body in the dumpster,

01:44:54.655 --> 01:44:55.956
he covered it with a blanket.

01:44:57.457 --> 01:45:01.028
Police questioned neighbors to find out if they'd seen anyone suspicious.

01:45:02.029 --> 01:45:04.197
No one had seen anything out of the ordinary.

01:45:05.999 --> 01:45:08.602
Homicide detectives came to the scene to investigate.

01:45:11.838 --> 01:45:14.408
An emergency unit arrived to retrieve the body.

01:45:24.451 --> 01:45:26.887
The victim was identified as Joanne Sweets,

01:45:27.120 --> 01:45:27.921
a prostitute.

01:45:28.722 --> 01:45:30.624
She had been raped and strangled to death,

01:45:30.991 --> 01:45:32.559
and several of her ribs were broken.

01:45:33.627 --> 01:45:35.962
That was the serial killer's calling card.

01:45:39.933 --> 01:45:41.868
Would the killer elude police again?

01:45:42.803 --> 01:45:44.671
It was going to be a tough case to crack.

01:45:45.772 --> 01:45:47.674
Since most of the victims were prostitutes,

01:45:48.041 --> 01:45:49.776
the murders weren't always reported,

01:45:50.477 --> 01:45:52.813
or the few eyewitnesses were unreliable.

01:45:58.352 --> 01:45:59.086
But this time,

01:45:59.486 --> 01:46:02.723
the detectives were able to lift a fingerprint from the dumpster.

01:46:08.729 --> 01:46:15.869
Detective Dan Hatfield was part of a task force formed to stop the horrid wave of killings before it went any further.

01:46:16.903 --> 01:46:17.471
Primarily,

01:46:18.105 --> 01:46:22.743
the whole focus of the task force was to look into women,

01:46:22.809 --> 01:46:24.010
primarily prostitutes,

01:46:24.111 --> 01:46:28.749
that had been found murdered here in the city of San Diego and also in the county.

01:46:29.249 --> 01:46:32.319
There was approximately 35 to 40 unsolved cases.

01:46:35.355 --> 01:46:37.424
The Joanne Sweets case was the latest.

01:46:37.958 --> 01:46:38.625
With any luck,

01:46:38.959 --> 01:46:40.026
it would be the last.

01:46:41.728 --> 01:46:44.998
Detectives believe the killer lived or worked in the neighborhood.

01:46:45.999 --> 01:46:47.134
Two other prostitutes'

01:46:47.167 --> 01:46:49.936
bodies had also been discovered in dumpsters nearby.

01:46:52.873 --> 01:47:00.947
We had Tara Simpson that was found in another dumpster that's adjacent to the Joanne Sweets case.

01:47:01.948 --> 01:47:04.551
The dumpster was located at the T of the alley.

01:47:06.720 --> 01:47:07.754
The early morning hours,

01:47:08.789 --> 01:47:09.823
police were called here.

01:47:10.557 --> 01:47:13.293
They found the garbage container fully engulfed.

01:47:13.660 --> 01:47:17.631
Fire department finds that there's a female in there and she is badly burned.

01:47:18.298 --> 01:47:22.102
A lot of the evidence was lost because of the fact that she was badly burned.

01:47:22.402 --> 01:47:23.437
Several months after

01:47:23.870 --> 01:47:26.106
Tara Simpson's body was found here,

01:47:26.907 --> 01:47:34.848
we go up several blocks up the same alleyway at another dumpster was found the body of Trina Carpenter.

01:47:35.949 --> 01:47:39.085
The carpenter had also been manually strangled.

01:47:40.220 --> 01:47:43.690
She was wrapped in a green duffel bag at that time.

01:47:45.091 --> 01:47:49.529
Hatfield was sure the same man was behind the deaths of all the women.

01:47:50.597 --> 01:47:53.099
But the investigation turned up no suspects.

01:47:54.167 --> 01:48:01.441
A manual search of fingerprints in police files failed to match the print found on the dumpster where Sweet's body was found.

01:48:02.576 --> 01:48:05.445
Whoever left the print didn't have a criminal record.

01:48:06.546 --> 01:48:08.315
The case went unsolved.

01:48:11.418 --> 01:48:12.319
Three years later,

01:48:12.719 --> 01:48:17.858
fingerprint expert Diane Donnelly joined the task force to work on the Joanne Sweets case.

01:48:18.992 --> 01:48:23.730
I was brought in on this case in 1989 at the request of homicide.

01:48:24.197 --> 01:48:32.506
And this is one of the cases that they had asked to go back and re-examine some of the evidence to see if there was anything else we could do at this point.

01:48:33.673 --> 01:48:42.816
She learned that fingerprint experts had already tried using a chemical called gentian violet to lift prints from the masking tape that held the garbage bags around the body.

01:48:45.752 --> 01:48:49.422
The process can expose fingerprints left on sticky surfaces.

01:48:50.590 --> 01:48:54.060
When a finger touches the adhesive side of tape and is removed,

01:48:54.528 --> 01:48:56.396
skin cells remain behind.

01:48:57.697 --> 01:49:00.200
The gentian violet stains those cells,

01:49:00.534 --> 01:49:01.501
revealing the print.

01:49:04.204 --> 01:49:06.740
Experts repeated the process over and over,

01:49:07.207 --> 01:49:09.109
but couldn't raise a single print.

01:49:17.150 --> 01:49:17.350
Then,

01:49:17.884 --> 01:49:20.287
shortly after Donnelly joined the task force,

01:49:20.887 --> 01:49:23.423
she and San Diego detectives received a break.

01:49:24.491 --> 01:49:31.898
They decided to make another attempt to identify the print from the dumpster using a new computerized fingerprint matching system.

01:49:34.134 --> 01:49:38.572
Several suspects were considered and dismissed before a match was made.

01:49:39.873 --> 01:49:43.510
The prints belonged to a man named Brian Maurice Jones.

01:49:44.477 --> 01:49:46.446
At the time of the San Diego murders,

01:49:46.646 --> 01:49:47.814
he'd never been arrested.

01:49:48.815 --> 01:49:49.316
Since then,

01:49:49.783 --> 01:49:51.484
Jones had been convicted for rape,

01:49:51.785 --> 01:49:52.152
robbery,

01:49:52.352 --> 01:49:53.820
and kidnapping a prostitute.

01:49:54.854 --> 01:49:58.592
He became the prime suspect in the murder of Joanne Sweets.

01:50:03.096 --> 01:50:07.167
But detectives knew the print from the dumpster wasn't enough to make a case.

01:50:07.934 --> 01:50:09.269
Jones would have an alibi.

01:50:10.870 --> 01:50:15.609
His mother lived in a building adjacent to the alley where Sweet's body had been found.

01:50:16.576 --> 01:50:22.015
And of course you could logically assume that his defense would be that he had taken out his mother's trash.

01:50:22.549 --> 01:50:24.384
So we needed something more concrete,

01:50:24.417 --> 01:50:26.353
that proverbial nail in the coffin,

01:50:27.287 --> 01:50:33.460
to link him to this murder and maybe some of the other murders of the women in San Diego.

01:50:35.595 --> 01:50:36.529
Even without a print,

01:50:36.963 --> 01:50:42.102
Dan Hatfield had a strong hunch that Jones had murdered Joanne Sweets and the others.

01:50:43.536 --> 01:50:45.038
According to Hatfield's scenario,

01:50:45.672 --> 01:50:49.209
Jones most likely cruised the boulevard looking for victims.

01:50:50.310 --> 01:50:54.547
He'd pick a prostitute and take her to his mother's apartment while she was at work.

01:50:56.883 --> 01:50:58.551
He'd act like a typical client,

01:50:59.152 --> 01:51:01.287
but the evening would culminate in murder.

01:51:16.803 --> 01:51:17.237
Afterward,

01:51:17.537 --> 01:51:20.340
he'd wrap the body up and take it out to the dumpster,

01:51:20.807 --> 01:51:22.375
like he was taking out the trash.

01:51:24.310 --> 01:51:26.980
Jones was still in prison for lesser crimes,

01:51:27.714 --> 01:51:30.316
but he'd be eligible for parole in 10 years.

01:51:31.451 --> 01:51:34.287
If Hatfield could link him to the Joanne Sweets murder,

01:51:34.754 --> 01:51:37.257
he'd make sure Jones would never get out.

01:51:39.059 --> 01:51:39.693
I believe when

01:51:40.560 --> 01:51:41.661
Mr. Jones dumped

01:51:42.862 --> 01:51:43.396
Joanne Sweets'

01:51:43.430 --> 01:51:44.698
body in the dumpster,

01:51:45.565 --> 01:51:49.269
he probably felt he could get away with it since he got away with the other two murders.

01:51:52.872 --> 01:51:54.407
In their effort to prove Jones'

01:51:54.541 --> 01:51:54.841
guilt,

01:51:55.275 --> 01:51:59.779
the detectives would pin their hopes on the latest method of fingerprint technology.

01:52:06.086 --> 01:52:07.721
Stalled for three years,

01:52:08.088 --> 01:52:12.726
the murder investigation of Joanne Sweets got a jump start in 1992.

01:52:14.527 --> 01:52:19.299
Detectives once again focused on the garbage bags the killer used to wrap his victim.

01:52:20.934 --> 01:52:21.901
Six years earlier,

01:52:22.335 --> 01:52:24.337
no prints were found on the bags.

01:52:25.171 --> 01:52:29.309
But Dan Hatfield and Diane Donnelly were sure the prints were there.

01:52:32.011 --> 01:52:34.781
At the time that I was investigating these cases,

01:52:34.848 --> 01:52:36.850
it was my feeling that there were,

01:52:36.850 --> 01:52:37.250
in fact,

01:52:37.283 --> 01:52:38.885
latent prints on the garbage bags.

01:52:39.052 --> 01:52:41.454
We were just not using the right technique.

01:52:42.122 --> 01:52:42.989
I checked around.

01:52:43.623 --> 01:52:45.058
I talked with the FBI.

01:52:45.992 --> 01:52:53.633
What they told me is that there was a technique that was being used in England and also in Canada with the Royal Canadian Mounted Police,

01:52:53.933 --> 01:52:56.035
a technique called vacuum metal deposition,

01:52:56.903 --> 01:52:58.872
at which point I called Canada.

01:52:59.739 --> 01:53:01.708
I found out that they were in fact using this

01:53:02.142 --> 01:53:08.815
technique to lift latent prints from plastics and that they were more than happy to do our case.

01:53:10.583 --> 01:53:15.488
Donnelly took the evidence to the laboratories of the Royal Canadian Mounted Police in Ottawa.

01:53:16.890 --> 01:53:20.260
She was hopeful but the prints were six years old.

01:53:21.327 --> 01:53:24.264
Would they have degraded too much for the process to work?

01:53:25.498 --> 01:53:28.368
They were more than willing and happy to assist me in this matter,

01:53:28.468 --> 01:53:33.807
but they were not too optimistic about obtaining any identifiable imprints.

01:53:35.942 --> 01:53:36.609
The process,

01:53:37.010 --> 01:53:39.145
known as vacuum metal deposition,

01:53:39.512 --> 01:53:40.814
was developed in the U.S.,

01:53:41.414 --> 01:53:44.350
but most jurisdictions don't have the money to utilize it.

01:53:45.251 --> 01:53:47.821
It's used more widely in Europe and Canada.

01:53:49.322 --> 01:53:51.524
Its main application is on plastics.

01:53:53.593 --> 01:53:57.697
But for fingerprint expert Pat Letournes of the Royal Canadian Mounted Police,

01:53:58.298 --> 01:54:02.335
it's a versatile process that works when all other techniques have failed.

01:54:04.103 --> 01:54:07.707
It's possible to get fingerprints on things like magazine paper,

01:54:08.575 --> 01:54:09.209
paper towel,

01:54:10.210 --> 01:54:10.577
tissue,

01:54:10.944 --> 01:54:13.112
very fine exhibits.

01:54:14.113 --> 01:54:16.850
It has limited application in some of those things,

01:54:16.916 --> 01:54:19.285
but really when there's no other way to do it,

01:54:19.853 --> 01:54:20.353
it works.

01:54:22.355 --> 01:54:24.524
If we take the best exhibit,

01:54:25.425 --> 01:54:27.493
the solid plastic type of exhibit,

01:54:27.527 --> 01:54:32.498
it's a matter of keeping that clean and then placing it inside the work holder of the chamber.

01:54:33.933 --> 01:54:35.335
Handling the evidence carefully,

01:54:35.869 --> 01:54:37.637
Letourneuse places it in the chamber.

01:54:39.839 --> 01:54:43.142
A few milligrams of gold are deposited on a heating element.

01:54:50.717 --> 01:54:52.051
Once the chamber is sealed,

01:54:52.518 --> 01:54:53.920
pumps create a vacuum.

01:54:56.022 --> 01:54:57.257
Once it's within a vacuum,

01:54:57.357 --> 01:54:59.058
the gold source is heated,

01:54:59.692 --> 01:55:01.361
and that heat is melting the metal.

01:55:01.928 --> 01:55:03.930
The metal would almost liquefy,

01:55:04.030 --> 01:55:04.330
boil,

01:55:04.564 --> 01:55:07.100
and then you can compare it to steam,

01:55:07.166 --> 01:55:10.870
where it would go straight up and condense it on the surface that it hits.

01:55:12.538 --> 01:55:13.039
A thin,

01:55:13.306 --> 01:55:16.876
invisible layer coats the plastic surface of the garbage bag.

01:55:17.777 --> 01:55:19.812
If the bag contains no fingerprints,

01:55:20.179 --> 01:55:22.215
the layer of gold will be uniform.

01:55:23.349 --> 01:55:24.751
But if fingerprints are present,

01:55:25.251 --> 01:55:26.786
the gold will sink into them,

01:55:27.387 --> 01:55:30.156
leaving the oily ridges of the print uncoated.

01:55:37.797 --> 01:55:40.900
The process is then repeated with a few milligrams of zinc.

01:55:45.138 --> 01:55:45.939
Like the gold,

01:55:46.339 --> 01:55:48.908
the zinc vaporizes within the evidence chamber.

01:55:49.976 --> 01:55:52.512
It will recondense only onto other metals,

01:55:53.046 --> 01:55:56.015
so it will only cling to the previous layer of gold.

01:55:58.751 --> 01:56:03.356
But the zinc won't stick on the oily residue of the fingerprint where there's no gold.

01:56:04.357 --> 01:56:07.093
The result is a high contrast fingerprint.

01:56:15.468 --> 01:56:18.338
The bags were removed from the chamber and inspected.

01:56:19.372 --> 01:56:23.142
This moment would make or break the case against Jones.

01:56:24.610 --> 01:56:26.212
After six years in hiding,

01:56:26.579 --> 01:56:29.449
the prints on the bags finally became visible.

01:56:30.583 --> 01:56:31.150
And with them,

01:56:31.551 --> 01:56:34.787
a dead-end case cleared a major roadblock.

01:56:37.056 --> 01:56:38.725
We knew this exhibit was six years old.

01:56:39.125 --> 01:56:42.495
We knew it was involved in a homicide which made it a high-profile case.

01:56:43.096 --> 01:56:46.866
And it was quite exciting to see the latent when we pulled it out of the chamber.

01:56:48.701 --> 01:56:54.207
The latent prints found on the garbage bags matched the prints of Brian Maurice Jones.

01:56:55.174 --> 01:57:00.947
The vacuum metal deposition process enabled Hatfield and Donnelly to connect him to the murder.

01:57:02.281 --> 01:57:03.449
The nail and the casket were,

01:57:03.449 --> 01:57:03.950
in fact,

01:57:04.650 --> 01:57:11.958
the latent prints that were taken from the garbage bags and that came back matching Brian Jones without a doubt.

01:57:13.459 --> 01:57:17.630
There was no way for him to disprove the fact that these were somebody else's prints.

01:57:18.698 --> 01:57:19.899
That was a nail in the coffin,

01:57:20.233 --> 01:57:21.200
as far as I'm concerned.

01:57:21.200 --> 01:57:24.504
In 1996,

01:57:24.904 --> 01:57:31.511
the state of California tried Brian Maurice Jones for the murder of Joanne Sweets and several related crimes.

01:57:32.245 --> 01:57:34.480
He was convicted and sentenced to death.

01:57:36.249 --> 01:57:41.821
Advances in the science of fingerprint detection had solved a case that seemed all but hopeless.

01:57:44.657 --> 01:57:46.025
I feel very good about it,

01:57:46.025 --> 01:57:49.562
the fact that even though these victims were prostitutes,

01:57:49.896 --> 01:57:50.897
they were people also.

01:57:52.298 --> 01:57:52.665
And

01:57:53.099 --> 01:57:54.434
I think with his conviction,

01:57:55.134 --> 01:57:56.869
I believe that they had their day in court,

01:57:58.071 --> 01:57:59.439
and justice was served.

01:58:01.908 --> 01:58:05.244
The vacuum deposition process raised Brian Jones'

01:58:05.311 --> 01:58:07.480
fingerprints and secured his conviction.

01:58:08.381 --> 01:58:10.983
But it was a computer that first singled him out.

01:58:13.953 --> 01:58:15.421
In another California case,

01:58:15.755 --> 01:58:20.560
detectives used computer technology to pursue a killer across three decades.

01:58:28.367 --> 01:58:28.968
On October 2,

01:58:28.968 --> 01:58:31.704
1963,

01:58:31.704 --> 01:58:35.241
Thora Rose was spending a quiet evening alone in her apartment in Hollywood,

01:58:35.341 --> 01:58:35.942
California.

01:58:37.877 --> 01:58:40.046
She had rented the apartment just a month earlier,

01:58:40.446 --> 01:58:41.981
after separating from her husband,

01:58:42.482 --> 01:58:44.884
and was slowly adjusting to life on her own.

01:58:48.254 --> 01:58:49.655
Rose worked as a waitress,

01:58:50.056 --> 01:58:52.658
and kept mainly to herself in her free time.

01:58:55.061 --> 01:58:58.631
The ground floor apartment was considered to be in a safe neighborhood,

01:58:59.432 --> 01:59:01.200
even for a woman who lived alone.

01:59:06.272 --> 01:59:06.939
But that night...

01:59:07.440 --> 01:59:09.008
Someone invaded that safety,

01:59:09.809 --> 01:59:12.278
and Thora Rose became his target.

01:59:16.716 --> 01:59:19.986
He waited in the darkness as she settled in for the evening.

01:59:26.125 --> 01:59:27.059
When her lights went out,

01:59:27.393 --> 01:59:28.461
he made his move.

01:59:37.236 --> 01:59:38.905
As Rose drifted off to sleep,

01:59:39.238 --> 01:59:43.776
he pried open a window over the kitchen sink and crawled into her apartment.

02:00:02.061 --> 02:00:02.228
Once

02:00:02.295 --> 02:00:02.895
inside,

02:00:03.196 --> 02:00:06.299
he slipped through the kitchen and crept toward the bedroom.

02:00:13.906 --> 02:00:14.640
When he got there,

02:00:15.141 --> 02:00:15.808
he attacked.

02:00:18.844 --> 02:00:20.213
After a violent struggle,

02:00:20.646 --> 02:00:21.447
Thora Rose,

02:00:21.881 --> 02:00:22.715
age 43,

02:00:23.316 --> 02:00:23.816
was dead.

02:00:27.920 --> 02:00:30.356
When Rose failed to come to work the next day,

02:00:30.723 --> 02:00:32.124
her employer telephoned her,

02:00:32.425 --> 02:00:33.693
but got no response.

02:00:34.560 --> 02:00:35.194
Concerned,

02:00:35.461 --> 02:00:36.529
he called the police.

02:00:37.930 --> 02:00:38.864
When they arrived,

02:00:39.198 --> 02:00:41.901
they found Thora Rose's body in the bedroom.

02:00:54.413 --> 02:00:55.815
Police questioned neighbors,

02:00:56.148 --> 02:00:59.685
but no one had seen anyone enter or leave Rose's apartment.

02:01:02.989 --> 02:01:04.156
Nobody heard a thing.

02:01:09.095 --> 02:01:14.033
It was one of the worst crimes the quiet Hollywood neighborhood had ever experienced.

02:01:16.769 --> 02:01:18.604
Almost 35 years later,

02:01:19.038 --> 02:01:23.342
Los Angeles police detective Mike McDonough visits the scene of the crime.

02:01:24.810 --> 02:01:29.382
Hollywood back then was a completely different place as it is today.

02:01:29.415 --> 02:01:29.682
I mean,

02:01:29.782 --> 02:01:31.584
when you think Hollywood back in 63,

02:01:31.584 --> 02:01:33.519
it was still the movie industry,

02:01:34.887 --> 02:01:37.423
still a lot of single-family residences here,

02:01:37.923 --> 02:01:39.525
a couple of apartment buildings,

02:01:39.959 --> 02:01:41.427
a completely different world.

02:01:43.396 --> 02:01:47.300
The crime rate was practically nothing compared to what it is to today.

02:01:48.234 --> 02:01:48.834
Hollywood now,

02:01:48.868 --> 02:01:50.036
we're averaging anywhere from

02:01:50.636 --> 02:01:52.071
50 to 60 homicides a year.

02:01:52.672 --> 02:01:53.939
Back in 1963,

02:01:53.939 --> 02:01:54.473
they had four.

02:01:56.742 --> 02:01:58.544
The murder caused a major stir.

02:02:00.613 --> 02:02:05.151
The Homicide Division of the Los Angeles Police Department gave the case top priority.

02:02:07.353 --> 02:02:07.853
At first,

02:02:08.187 --> 02:02:10.556
just two detectives were assigned to the case,

02:02:10.990 --> 02:02:12.958
but the number quickly rose to six.

02:02:14.260 --> 02:02:14.760
Eventually,

02:02:15.161 --> 02:02:19.398
32 uniformed officers and two sergeants joined the investigation.

02:02:20.633 --> 02:02:22.935
They canvassed the neighborhood for a suspect.

02:02:24.370 --> 02:02:25.404
Inside the apartment,

02:02:25.671 --> 02:02:27.573
experts dusted for fingerprints.

02:02:28.974 --> 02:02:33.079
There was palm prints and fingerprints inside the kitchen and throughout the house.

02:02:34.380 --> 02:02:37.650
There's approximately 27 fingerprints that were lifted inside the residence,

02:02:37.683 --> 02:02:40.086
leading from the front window here into the bedroom.

02:02:42.788 --> 02:02:44.023
The police officers worked hard to find the fingerprints.

02:02:44.223 --> 02:02:45.858
The neighborhood found nothing.

02:02:46.859 --> 02:02:50.262
It was up to the fingerprint experts alone to solve the case.

02:02:51.330 --> 02:02:54.066
With the long trail of fingerprints left behind,

02:02:54.500 --> 02:02:57.136
the detectives were certain they would catch the murderer.

02:02:58.571 --> 02:03:00.206
Their confidence was well founded.

02:03:01.073 --> 02:03:02.641
For more than 100 years,

02:03:03.042 --> 02:03:08.447
fingerprinting has proven to be one of the most effective ways to pin criminals to crimes.

02:03:09.515 --> 02:03:11.617
The science goes back to 1880,

02:03:12.218 --> 02:03:20.292
when Scottish physician Henry Falls suggested that ridge patterns on the fingers and hands could be useful in identifying criminals.

02:03:21.127 --> 02:03:22.294
In 1901,

02:03:22.728 --> 02:03:24.497
Scotland Yard adopted the idea,

02:03:24.964 --> 02:03:26.866
and the rest of the world soon followed.

02:03:29.235 --> 02:03:31.470
Fingerprinting works for two reasons.

02:03:32.271 --> 02:03:32.638
First,

02:03:33.072 --> 02:03:35.541
no two people share print patterns.

02:03:36.242 --> 02:03:36.776
And second,

02:03:37.243 --> 02:03:40.746
a person's fingerprints remain unchanged throughout life.

02:03:43.416 --> 02:03:48.721
The skin of human fingers and hands have raised patterns called friction ridges,

02:03:49.255 --> 02:03:51.190
which help us grip objects more firmly.

02:03:52.992 --> 02:03:57.163
They're constantly coated with a film of perspiration from tiny pores.

02:03:58.497 --> 02:03:59.198
The curves,

02:03:59.498 --> 02:03:59.865
loops,

02:03:59.999 --> 02:04:04.637
and other characteristics of the ridges can occur in billions of combinations.

02:04:06.238 --> 02:04:07.173
At a crime scene,

02:04:07.540 --> 02:04:11.043
the perpetrator may leave noticeable prints if he touched blood,

02:04:11.544 --> 02:04:11.944
grease,

02:04:12.211 --> 02:04:13.712
or another dark substance.

02:04:14.647 --> 02:04:16.115
If he touched something soft,

02:04:16.348 --> 02:04:16.816
like putty,

02:04:17.283 --> 02:04:19.652
the fingerprints may be impressed on its surface.

02:04:22.021 --> 02:04:24.457
But the majority of fingerprints are invisible,

02:04:25.090 --> 02:04:26.892
known as latent fingerprints.

02:04:28.093 --> 02:04:29.895
They're made of about 98%

02:04:29.962 --> 02:04:31.564
perspiration and 2%

02:04:31.997 --> 02:04:32.631
body oil.

02:04:33.799 --> 02:04:36.635
We leave them on virtually everything we touch.

02:04:38.804 --> 02:04:40.105
Like film in a camera,

02:04:40.606 --> 02:04:42.508
they must be developed to be seen.

02:04:44.643 --> 02:04:52.184
The fingerprint experts at Los Angeles Police Department's fingerprint lab have long relied on powders to make latent prints visible.

02:04:53.252 --> 02:04:57.490
It's been one of the most common and effective methods since fingerprinting began.

02:04:59.325 --> 02:05:02.027
When lightly applied with a camel hair brush,

02:05:02.661 --> 02:05:05.130
powder adheres to the moisture in the fingerprint,

02:05:05.664 --> 02:05:07.766
providing a finely detailed image.

02:05:09.335 --> 02:05:15.641
The detective then lifts the print using a strip of clear tape and places it on a card with his initials,

02:05:16.041 --> 02:05:16.609
the time,

02:05:17.142 --> 02:05:17.443
date,

02:05:17.576 --> 02:05:18.777
and location of the print.

02:05:19.979 --> 02:05:24.783
This detailed information is vital if the print will be used as evidence in court.

02:05:27.753 --> 02:05:34.260
After the fingerprint experts working on the Thora Rose case lifted the finger and palm prints from her Hollywood apartment,

02:05:34.927 --> 02:05:37.429
they had to prove they belonged to the perpetrator.

02:05:38.831 --> 02:05:42.134
There's always a possibility they could belong to someone else.

02:05:44.970 --> 02:05:51.143
Detectives obtained what are called elimination prints from everyone who had contact with Thora Rose.

02:05:52.011 --> 02:05:54.380
They were able to contact those people,

02:05:54.446 --> 02:05:55.714
the restaurants,

02:05:55.814 --> 02:05:56.682
places she worked.

02:05:57.049 --> 02:05:58.384
They fingerprinted everyone that was there.

02:05:58.918 --> 02:05:59.785
They also went as far.

02:05:59.985 --> 02:06:01.487
as local delivery boys,

02:06:01.487 --> 02:06:02.321
as far as serving,

02:06:02.354 --> 02:06:03.255
delivering chicken,

02:06:04.023 --> 02:06:04.623
mail people,

02:06:05.624 --> 02:06:07.426
newspaper people.

02:06:07.560 --> 02:06:09.728
Anyone that had any contact with this place,

02:06:09.728 --> 02:06:10.362
they checked out.

02:06:11.864 --> 02:06:13.866
After all other persons were eliminated,

02:06:14.266 --> 02:06:16.869
detectives drew the only possible conclusion.

02:06:17.503 --> 02:06:19.338
The prints belonged to the killer.

02:06:21.473 --> 02:06:27.846
Now they could be sent to the lab to be compared by fingerprint examiners to prints of criminals in their files.

02:06:29.415 --> 02:06:32.618
Whenever police make an arrest for even the smallest infraction,

02:06:33.118 --> 02:06:35.721
they require the arrested person to be fingerprinted.

02:06:36.822 --> 02:06:38.390
The prints are kept on file,

02:06:38.924 --> 02:06:39.858
and in some cases,

02:06:40.092 --> 02:06:41.961
sent to other police jurisdictions.

02:06:43.028 --> 02:06:45.731
If the suspect is ever involved in another crime,

02:06:46.131 --> 02:06:48.534
his prints will be available for comparison.

02:06:50.336 --> 02:06:54.540
The traditional way of recording fingerprints is the ink and roll method.

02:06:55.741 --> 02:06:57.943
Each finger is rolled on an ink pad,

02:06:58.510 --> 02:07:02.648
then impressed on a card with the arrested person's name and personal data.

02:07:03.716 --> 02:07:06.485
The document is then added to the fingerprint files.

02:07:08.754 --> 02:07:09.154
Recently,

02:07:09.555 --> 02:07:13.025
some jurisdictions have begun scanning fingerprints into a computer.

02:07:14.259 --> 02:07:19.098
The scanner creates a digital image of the prints so they can be added to the database.

02:07:19.999 --> 02:07:22.668
A beam of light replaces the ink pad.

02:07:24.036 --> 02:07:24.637
Either way,

02:07:25.004 --> 02:07:32.177
the matching process begins when the examiner compares prints from the crime scene with prints from police files.

02:07:36.148 --> 02:07:40.552
Comparing fingerprints is much the same today as in 1963.

02:07:41.687 --> 02:07:43.956
The examiner must look for matching points.

02:07:44.023 --> 02:07:44.890
of identification.

02:07:46.625 --> 02:07:49.428
The friction ridges arrange themselves into arches,

02:07:49.795 --> 02:07:50.162
loops,

02:07:50.329 --> 02:07:51.130
and whorls.

02:07:51.964 --> 02:07:53.499
Sometimes they end abruptly.

02:07:54.266 --> 02:07:56.001
Sometimes they split in two.

02:07:58.070 --> 02:08:02.007
The examiner considers all these patterns when making an identification.

02:08:03.308 --> 02:08:04.476
If enough of them match,

02:08:04.977 --> 02:08:08.480
then he can be certain he's looking at the prints of the same person.

02:08:10.649 --> 02:08:11.984
In the Thora Rose case,

02:08:12.351 --> 02:08:16.121
Los Angeles detectives reviewed all the fingerprints in their files.

02:08:17.456 --> 02:08:18.524
When none matched,

02:08:18.924 --> 02:08:24.163
they sent a detective to the state capitol in Sacramento to expand the search statewide.

02:08:25.364 --> 02:08:27.066
He scoured every file,

02:08:27.766 --> 02:08:30.636
looking at a staggering 30,000 fingerprints.

02:08:31.570 --> 02:08:32.871
The labor took months,

02:08:33.939 --> 02:08:36.041
but still nothing matched.

02:08:36.875 --> 02:08:37.142
I mean,

02:08:37.409 --> 02:08:43.482
it's a point that they put in an unbelievable man hours of time on this case.

02:08:44.383 --> 02:08:47.352
And even with all that they have done,

02:08:47.386 --> 02:08:51.924
which is probably thousands and thousands of percent more than what we could do today with our crimes,

02:08:52.224 --> 02:08:53.792
they still weren't able to come up with anything.

02:08:55.160 --> 02:08:58.197
Even though the killer had left behind many fingerprints,

02:08:58.731 --> 02:09:02.201
the detectives couldn't match them to anyone with a police record.

02:09:03.168 --> 02:09:04.770
The case was unsolved.

02:09:05.637 --> 02:09:06.939
The files were shelled.

02:09:07.873 --> 02:09:10.609
And the murderer of Thora Rose went free.

02:09:12.444 --> 02:09:17.382
Thirty years would pass before time and technology would flush him out.

02:09:22.087 --> 02:09:24.823
Three decades after the murder of Thora Rose,

02:09:25.157 --> 02:09:29.394
a new computerized system of fingerprint comparison went online.

02:09:30.696 --> 02:09:33.232
The Automated Fingerprint Identification System,

02:09:33.732 --> 02:09:34.366
or APHIS,

02:09:34.833 --> 02:09:38.370
promised to revolutionize the field of fingerprint identification.

02:09:43.742 --> 02:09:47.746
It matches prints in a fraction of the time it took using the old method.

02:09:52.885 --> 02:09:59.625
Fingerprint examiner Donald Keer was one of the first at the Los Angeles Police Department to put APHIS to use.

02:10:00.759 --> 02:10:05.230
This fingerprint system takes the time to get used to.

02:10:05.297 --> 02:10:05.764
It was new.

02:10:06.064 --> 02:10:07.533
It takes time to realize it,

02:10:07.533 --> 02:10:08.801
and we had a lot of crimes to solve.

02:10:09.668 --> 02:10:16.742
Keir and his colleagues first used APHIS to match prints collected from current crimes against those in APHIS's files.

02:10:17.709 --> 02:10:25.117
Then they tried an experiment to see if the system could solve old cases by matching previously unmatched prints.

02:10:27.920 --> 02:10:31.023
They chose 50 old homicide cases to test.

02:10:33.125 --> 02:10:36.195
Could APHIS breathe new life into dead cases?

02:10:37.029 --> 02:10:37.729
To find out,

02:10:38.096 --> 02:10:41.266
Keir went to the archives in the basement of the police department.

02:10:47.005 --> 02:10:47.339
There,

02:10:47.739 --> 02:10:50.142
under the dust of 30 years or more,

02:10:50.542 --> 02:10:53.846
stood shelves brimming with old fingerprint files.

02:10:54.813 --> 02:10:57.382
They were gathered from all manner of crimes,

02:10:57.883 --> 02:10:58.717
some solved,

02:10:59.117 --> 02:10:59.785
some not.

02:11:00.586 --> 02:11:04.623
One of the files he pulled contained prints from the Thora Rose murder.

02:11:05.757 --> 02:11:07.559
It was the oldest case selected.

02:11:08.594 --> 02:11:13.198
The chance of finding a suspect after almost 30 years seemed remote.

02:11:14.666 --> 02:11:18.737
But with millions of prints added to police files since 1963,

02:11:19.538 --> 02:11:23.342
and the ability of the APHIS system to compare them at lightning speed,

02:11:24.042 --> 02:11:25.744
detectives had a glimmer of hope.

02:11:27.980 --> 02:11:28.547
But first,

02:11:28.847 --> 02:11:31.450
the prints from the Rose case had to be prepared.

02:11:32.985 --> 02:11:35.454
Before APHIS can recognize any fingerprint,

02:11:35.854 --> 02:11:39.958
an examiner must photograph it at five times its normal size.

02:11:42.561 --> 02:11:45.931
In contrast to prints taken from a suspect at the police station,

02:11:46.565 --> 02:11:51.470
the ridges and patterns of most prints from a crime scene are faint and indistinct.

02:11:52.638 --> 02:11:56.241
The examiner must carefully enhance the pattern on tracing paper.

02:11:57.509 --> 02:11:58.110
Otherwise,

02:11:58.377 --> 02:12:00.946
the computer scanner will be unable to read it.

02:12:02.214 --> 02:12:07.152
Any place where I'm looping it off is where a ridge in the fingerprint pattern ends.

02:12:08.654 --> 02:12:13.292
And we want to make sure those are really clear because that's what the computer uses for a search.

02:12:13.792 --> 02:12:16.128
They're called minutiae or characteristics.

02:12:16.862 --> 02:12:20.632
I check it frequently to see if I'm missing anything,

02:12:21.466 --> 02:12:23.468
go back over what I've been doing here.

02:12:24.670 --> 02:12:26.605
Where the latent print is unreadable,

02:12:26.972 --> 02:12:30.509
the examiner must hazard a guess as to line and detail.

02:12:31.944 --> 02:12:34.079
The tracing is scanned into the computer.

02:12:35.147 --> 02:12:42.154
The examiner cleans up any indistinct lines on the screen and identifies notable characteristics of the latent print.

02:12:42.154 --> 02:12:44.022
The computer will use the examiner to identify the latent print.

02:12:44.056 --> 02:12:45.958
use these as a frame of reference.

02:12:50.529 --> 02:12:53.031
APHIS then begins the matching process.

02:12:55.801 --> 02:12:58.837
The computer looks at several areas of the unknown print.

02:12:59.938 --> 02:13:03.342
It then compares these points against prints in its database.

02:13:05.544 --> 02:13:09.614
It ranks each print according to how closely it matches the unknown print.

02:13:10.449 --> 02:13:11.183
In another room,

02:13:11.616 --> 02:13:17.689
the massive APHIS mainframe searches through millions of digitized fingerprints looking for a match.

02:13:18.757 --> 02:13:19.858
In less than an hour,

02:13:20.392 --> 02:13:23.428
it completes a job that would ordinarily take months.

02:13:25.764 --> 02:13:25.998
Then,

02:13:26.365 --> 02:13:28.600
the system delivers the closest matches.

02:13:31.770 --> 02:13:35.173
But it's up to the examiner to make the final match by eye.

02:13:36.575 --> 02:13:41.880
The prints identified by APHIS are compared side by side with the suspect's print.

02:13:41.980 --> 02:13:43.749
There can be a lot of things that match,

02:13:43.849 --> 02:13:47.619
but if there's something that you know is pretty obviously a real minutia point,

02:13:47.719 --> 02:13:50.689
like this one was a pretty big one right here,

02:13:50.689 --> 02:13:52.391
a little short line,

02:13:52.924 --> 02:13:54.593
and it looks like that might be it there,

02:13:55.160 --> 02:13:58.130
but over next to it was a place where another ridge ended.

02:13:59.264 --> 02:14:00.866
There's nothing like that over here,

02:14:01.800 --> 02:14:04.069
so I would probably disregard that one.

02:14:05.270 --> 02:14:07.939
The AFIS system has had remarkable results.

02:14:08.774 --> 02:14:10.409
During its first year of operation,

02:14:10.842 --> 02:14:15.547
San Francisco police were able to clear 816 unsolved cases,

02:14:16.014 --> 02:14:18.083
including 52 homicides.

02:14:20.919 --> 02:14:25.223
Los Angeles police hoped for similar success with their unsolved cases.

02:14:26.191 --> 02:14:27.459
They weren't disappointed.

02:14:28.894 --> 02:14:32.230
Soon after they entered the fingerprints from the Thora Rose case,

02:14:32.764 --> 02:14:33.932
APHIS made a hit.

02:14:35.067 --> 02:14:37.102
The computer produced three suspects,

02:14:37.769 --> 02:14:40.238
among them a man named Vernon Robinson.

02:14:40.238 --> 02:14:42.874
In 1963,

02:14:43.375 --> 02:14:44.910
Robinson hadn't been arrested,

02:14:45.377 --> 02:14:47.212
so his fingerprints weren't on file.

02:14:48.313 --> 02:14:50.649
But he'd been arrested a number of times since then,

02:14:51.216 --> 02:14:53.351
so his prints were part of police records.

02:14:56.421 --> 02:14:59.624
Detectives using APHIS fingered him as a suspect.

02:15:01.259 --> 02:15:04.196
Detective Mike McDonough headed the new investigation.

02:15:05.897 --> 02:15:07.499
My main concern was to see if Mr.

02:15:07.566 --> 02:15:11.269
Robinson should have been there or not.

02:15:11.937 --> 02:15:21.379
I wanted to make sure that he wasn't one of the detectives or a police officer to see and he wasn't a paramedic or that he wasn't for some reason a friend of Ms.

02:15:21.480 --> 02:15:22.180
Robinson's,

02:15:23.415 --> 02:15:25.083
that fingerprints just happened to be there.

02:15:26.551 --> 02:15:28.887
When all other possibilities were eliminated,

02:15:29.387 --> 02:15:33.925
McDonough concluded that Robinson was the likely killer of Thora Rose.

02:15:35.961 --> 02:15:36.428
With that,

02:15:37.829 --> 02:15:40.398
our fingerprint people obtained the additional fingerprints,

02:15:40.866 --> 02:15:42.234
started hand-searching them,

02:15:42.734 --> 02:15:46.138
physically checking the fingerprints from the crime scene against Mr.

02:15:46.171 --> 02:15:47.105
Robinson's prints,

02:15:47.706 --> 02:15:49.407
and everyone is coming right back to Mr.

02:15:49.441 --> 02:15:49.908
Robinson.

02:15:50.041 --> 02:15:50.275
I mean,

02:15:50.275 --> 02:15:50.942
at this point,

02:15:51.309 --> 02:15:52.410
there was no doubt about it.

02:15:54.412 --> 02:15:57.182
Los Angeles police tracked Robinson to Minneapolis,

02:15:57.282 --> 02:15:57.782
Minnesota,

02:15:58.383 --> 02:16:02.521
where he was now a family man with a management job in a maintenance company.

02:16:06.324 --> 02:16:07.959
He denied committing the crime,

02:16:08.426 --> 02:16:10.128
insisting that at the time of the murder,

02:16:10.395 --> 02:16:13.632
he was in San Diego at the naval base where he was stationed.

02:16:14.833 --> 02:16:19.371
But naval records indicated Robinson had completed his training by the date of the murder.

02:16:20.205 --> 02:16:22.207
His alibi was without support.

02:16:25.644 --> 02:16:26.811
What swayed the jury was,

02:16:26.845 --> 02:16:27.946
I mean the fingerprints are there.

02:16:28.013 --> 02:16:29.414
You cannot deny that.

02:16:29.447 --> 02:16:31.316
I mean we're not talking one or two fingerprints.

02:16:31.349 --> 02:16:31.950
We're talking

02:16:33.218 --> 02:16:33.952
20-some fingerprints.

02:16:34.019 --> 02:16:39.524
We're talking them at the point of entry through the entire house and right up to where the victim was discovered.

02:16:41.092 --> 02:16:42.827
After killing Thora Rose,

02:16:43.228 --> 02:16:47.265
Vernon Robinson managed to evade capture for almost 30 years.

02:16:48.433 --> 02:16:49.935
His life had changed,

02:16:50.335 --> 02:16:52.637
but his fingerprints remained the same.

02:16:53.538 --> 02:16:55.073
After they were matched with the blood of the two women,

02:16:56.675 --> 02:17:00.579
Robinson was convicted of murder and sentenced to life imprisonment.

02:17:02.380 --> 02:17:02.747
Apis,

02:17:03.181 --> 02:17:05.850
a phenomenal breakthrough in criminal identification,

02:17:06.251 --> 02:17:09.588
had finally obtained justice for Thora Rose.

02:17:11.756 --> 02:17:16.461
Though APHIS has dramatically improved the chances of matching fingerprints to criminals,

02:17:17.128 --> 02:17:19.798
it's useless without clear prints to work from.

02:17:21.266 --> 02:17:27.172
But crime scenes are often messy and criminals don't always leave their prints in convenient spots.

02:17:28.673 --> 02:17:29.841
In a case in Vermont,

02:17:30.208 --> 02:17:37.782
a murder investigation hinged on two fragile and ill-placed prints and one investigator's attempt to read them.

02:17:41.253 --> 02:17:43.455
On Memorial Day 1992,

02:17:44.055 --> 02:17:45.523
Len Michelson had a party.

02:17:46.825 --> 02:17:52.931
He and his friends were putting the cold Vermont winter behind them and kicking off the beginning of summer.

02:17:54.266 --> 02:17:58.003
But the celebration was nearly ruined by an uninvited guest.

02:17:59.437 --> 02:18:01.039
As the party was winding down,

02:18:01.473 --> 02:18:05.410
he tried to make off in Michelson's car with one of the kegs of beer.

02:18:06.611 --> 02:18:09.447
But three of Michelson's buddies caught him in the act.

02:18:10.448 --> 02:18:13.418
They chased him off the property and retrieved the keg.

02:18:18.857 --> 02:18:21.192
With the commotion over and the keg emptied,

02:18:21.660 --> 02:18:25.430
the three men decided to continue celebrating at a nearby tavern.

02:18:27.532 --> 02:18:28.933
Michelson stayed home.

02:18:33.638 --> 02:18:36.374
The group returned to the house 45 minutes later,

02:18:37.008 --> 02:18:38.310
still in high spirits.

02:18:39.277 --> 02:18:39.778
At first,

02:18:39.844 --> 02:18:42.480
they didn't notice their host was nowhere in sight.

02:18:43.481 --> 02:18:44.549
When they called out to him,

02:18:44.916 --> 02:18:45.650
he didn't answer.

02:18:46.551 --> 02:18:47.952
They assumed he went to bed.

02:18:49.020 --> 02:18:55.560
It wasn't until one of the men noticed something peculiar in another room that the horrible truth revealed itself.

02:18:57.062 --> 02:19:03.168
A ski pole that appeared to be sticking out of the floor was actually embedded in Michelson's skull.

02:19:05.804 --> 02:19:07.639
They called the Vermont State Police,

02:19:08.006 --> 02:19:09.140
who rushed to the scene.

02:19:16.715 --> 02:19:22.454
Michelson's friends couldn't believe the friend they'd laughed with a few hours ago now lay dead.

02:19:29.861 --> 02:19:31.563
While the police made their report,

02:19:32.030 --> 02:19:34.366
detectives scoured the house for clues.

02:19:38.937 --> 02:19:43.441
Their inspection of the brutal crime scene revealed a bloody knife in the kitchen sink.

02:19:44.642 --> 02:19:51.182
It looked like the victim had been stabbed several times before the ski pole was repeatedly jabbed into his skull.

02:19:55.186 --> 02:19:56.888
According to Sergeant Miles Heffernan,

02:19:57.288 --> 02:19:59.090
the victim didn't die easily.

02:20:02.026 --> 02:20:06.364
He was obviously involved in a struggle,

02:20:07.499 --> 02:20:09.200
had a lot of blood on his clothes.

02:20:09.367 --> 02:20:11.936
There was a lot of blood on the walls in the hallway.

02:20:12.537 --> 02:20:13.238
When questioned,

02:20:13.505 --> 02:20:19.944
the three men who found the body told the detectives about the person who tried to steal Michelson's car and the beer keg.

02:20:20.845 --> 02:20:22.514
His name was Robert Plant.

02:20:23.481 --> 02:20:28.686
He'd tagged along with one of the invited guests and grew surly as the evening progressed.

02:20:30.054 --> 02:20:33.391
The men recalled he wore white shoes with pink laces,

02:20:34.092 --> 02:20:37.162
the same shoes that were found near the barefoot corpse.

02:20:38.229 --> 02:20:41.266
The cowboy boots that Michelson had worn were missing.

02:20:42.500 --> 02:20:44.302
As police continued to investigate,

02:20:44.602 --> 02:20:49.040
a call came through about a car that had run off the road less than a mile away.

02:20:50.208 --> 02:20:53.178
A neighbor named Robert Salzman made the report.

02:20:54.412 --> 02:20:54.646
Mr.

02:20:54.712 --> 02:20:59.717
Salzman was in the living room with his wife and child and heard the car go off the road.

02:21:00.218 --> 02:21:02.387
He came out and observed

02:21:02.921 --> 02:21:07.392
Robert Plant walking from the vehicle to the front porch of Mr.

02:21:07.459 --> 02:21:08.393
Salzman's residence.

02:21:09.761 --> 02:21:10.862
He had a discussion with

02:21:11.296 --> 02:21:11.996
Robert Plant.

02:21:12.697 --> 02:21:13.131
Initially,

02:21:14.299 --> 02:21:15.233
Plant seemed pleasant.

02:21:15.366 --> 02:21:17.101
He asked if he could get a wrecker.

02:21:19.671 --> 02:21:19.904
Mr.

02:21:19.938 --> 02:21:21.506
Salzman was agreeable.

02:21:23.241 --> 02:21:23.641
But then,

02:21:23.942 --> 02:21:26.244
Plant became aggressive and broke a window.

02:21:27.445 --> 02:21:30.482
Salzman threw him off his property and called the police.

02:21:32.817 --> 02:21:36.621
They arrived within minutes and found the car on the side of the road.

02:21:37.722 --> 02:21:40.625
It matched the description of Glenn Michelson's vehicle,

02:21:41.459 --> 02:21:43.428
but Plant was nowhere in sight.

02:21:45.096 --> 02:21:45.597
Apparently,

02:21:45.830 --> 02:21:46.798
he fled on foot.

02:21:49.667 --> 02:21:52.537
Police searched the woods and found him in a short time,

02:21:52.837 --> 02:21:54.205
passed out under a tree.

02:22:02.080 --> 02:22:04.849
On his feet were Michelson's cowboy boots.

02:22:10.522 --> 02:22:13.958
He was taken to the station for questioning and booked for murder.

02:22:17.028 --> 02:22:17.729
On the surface,

02:22:17.929 --> 02:22:21.199
it seemed Heffernan had an open and shut case against him.

02:22:22.967 --> 02:22:24.602
But Plant denied the crime,

02:22:24.969 --> 02:22:26.838
and the police had no eyewitnesses.

02:22:27.872 --> 02:22:28.506
Theoretically,

02:22:28.873 --> 02:22:33.778
Plant could claim he had stolen Michelson's property after someone else committed the murder.

02:22:35.413 --> 02:22:39.817
The detectives would try to bolster their circumstantial case with forensic evidence,

02:22:40.351 --> 02:22:41.486
Plant's fingerprints.

02:22:42.921 --> 02:22:45.156
They knew prints lifted from walls,

02:22:45.356 --> 02:22:45.723
sinks,

02:22:45.857 --> 02:22:47.592
and drawers had little value,

02:22:48.059 --> 02:22:50.328
since Plant had been a guest at Michelson's party.

02:22:51.429 --> 02:22:56.801
But they found bloody prints on the grip of the ski pole and on a door frame near the body.

02:22:58.202 --> 02:23:01.239
If these prints could be identified as Robert Plant's,

02:23:01.773 --> 02:23:03.374
police would clinch their case.

02:23:05.710 --> 02:23:07.478
To help him make the identification,

02:23:08.046 --> 02:23:14.218
Heffernan called on fingerprint expert John Creighton of the Vermont Department of Public Safety's forensic lab.

02:23:15.520 --> 02:23:18.456
Because the prints were etched in the victim's dried blood,

02:23:18.923 --> 02:23:22.727
they were extremely incriminating and extremely fragile.

02:23:24.128 --> 02:23:27.932
Traditional methods of dusting with powder would not be effective.

02:23:29.867 --> 02:23:30.435
Fortunately,

02:23:30.902 --> 02:23:36.140
Creighton has a well-stocked arsenal with the means to recover difficult prints.

02:23:37.875 --> 02:23:41.512
How a print is raised depends on the kind of surface it's on.

02:23:43.514 --> 02:23:46.718
Basically there's two different types of evidence that come into the lab for fingerprinting.

02:23:47.151 --> 02:23:48.886
There's porous and non-porous evidence.

02:23:49.721 --> 02:23:52.991
The porous evidence is papers and cardboards and things of that nature,

02:23:53.358 --> 02:23:55.560
and the non-porous evidence is wood,

02:23:56.260 --> 02:23:56.861
plastics,

02:23:56.961 --> 02:23:57.261
metal,

02:23:57.795 --> 02:23:58.196
glass,

02:23:58.630 --> 02:23:59.397
things of that nature.

02:24:00.098 --> 02:24:04.636
So depending on what type of evidence it is will dictate what type of examination you do.

02:24:09.307 --> 02:24:14.045
Paper and other porous surfaces leave no moisture for powders to cling to.

02:24:15.446 --> 02:24:20.118
One classic method for raising prints from these surfaces is iodine fuming.

02:24:22.186 --> 02:24:25.056
Iodine crystals are placed inside a glass tube.

02:24:28.393 --> 02:24:31.763
The tube is then packed with fiberglass and copper sulfate.

02:24:40.805 --> 02:24:43.174
Breath passing through the crystals heats them,

02:24:43.608 --> 02:24:44.709
creating fumes.

02:24:45.810 --> 02:24:47.612
When the fumes reach the fingerprints,

02:24:48.046 --> 02:24:50.415
the iodine reacts with fatty oils,

02:24:50.782 --> 02:24:51.749
making them visible.

02:24:52.884 --> 02:24:59.290
A drawback of this method is that the prints will disappear in about 20 minutes when the iodine evaporates.

02:25:00.091 --> 02:25:01.959
They must be photographed after fuming,

02:25:02.393 --> 02:25:03.995
so police will have a record of them.

02:25:06.798 --> 02:25:12.503
Another way to find fingerprints on paper is to spray the surface with a chemical called ninhydrin.

02:25:15.640 --> 02:25:27.051
Ninhydrin is a spray or a compound that reacts to amino acids that are present in eccrine and sebaceous sweat deposited late in prints.

02:25:27.051 --> 02:25:30.188
The ninhydrin is sprayed on paper.

02:25:30.254 --> 02:25:39.097
onto porous material and is then catalyzed or the reaction is catalyzed by applying heat and moisture,

02:25:39.897 --> 02:25:41.332
generally by means of an iron.

02:25:42.200 --> 02:25:45.036
This develops the prints much more quickly,

02:25:45.837 --> 02:25:54.812
otherwise you'd have to set them in the dark and wait anywhere from 24 to possibly 72 hours for any latent impressions to develop that way.

02:25:55.980 --> 02:26:00.017
Because the amino acids in fingerprints take a long time to disappear,

02:26:00.785 --> 02:26:05.323
ninhydrin has been used to develop latent prints as old as 15 years.

02:26:07.091 --> 02:26:10.595
Super glue has also become a staple of fingerprint examiners.

02:26:11.462 --> 02:26:13.965
Technically called cyanoacrylate ester,

02:26:14.465 --> 02:26:17.535
it's used on non-porous surfaces like plastic,

02:26:17.902 --> 02:26:22.173
where fragile prints could be easily brushed away when powders are applied.

02:26:23.975 --> 02:26:27.478
Super glue is often used for developing prints inside a car.

02:26:28.780 --> 02:26:31.783
The glue is poured into a small container and heated.

02:26:32.784 --> 02:26:34.519
The car is closed up tightly.

02:26:35.820 --> 02:26:36.888
As the glue is heated,

02:26:37.321 --> 02:26:42.426
its fumes adhere to the moisture in latent fingerprints and fixes them in place.

02:26:45.863 --> 02:26:50.868
The examiner can then use traditional powders without the danger of destroying the print.

02:26:53.871 --> 02:26:57.175
The Glenn Michelson case posed a different set of problems.

02:26:58.142 --> 02:27:05.082
The bloody thumbprint on the doorframe near the victim's body was barely visible and too delicate to lift.

02:27:05.983 --> 02:27:08.753
Creighton asked detectives to remove the section of doorframe.

02:27:08.753 --> 02:27:14.559
doorframe bearing the print and send it to him so he could examine it in a more controlled environment

02:27:15.159 --> 02:27:23.234
Creighton's job was to make the print on the doorframe distinct without ruining it he could then compare it with Robert plants

02:27:25.870 --> 02:27:26.237
First,

02:27:26.470 --> 02:27:30.575
he took photographs so he would have a record of the evidence before the procedure.

02:27:31.776 --> 02:27:40.318
Items of evidence are photographed before any physical or chemical development takes place in order to...

02:27:40.952 --> 02:27:45.823
recover and preserve any existing latent detail that is present on the item.

02:27:47.091 --> 02:27:47.792
Afterwards,

02:27:48.960 --> 02:27:54.899
then we can do the various processes that are applied to developing the latent impression on that item.

02:27:58.469 --> 02:28:02.440
Creighton sprayed the doorframe with a stain called Amido Black.

02:28:03.374 --> 02:28:04.909
The chemical reacts with blood,

02:28:05.476 --> 02:28:08.246
darkening the print and making it easier to identify.

02:28:13.017 --> 02:28:15.219
The Amido Black is a protein stain.

02:28:15.486 --> 02:28:19.056
It stains the protein that is within the blood itself.

02:28:19.757 --> 02:28:25.162
So when the ridges or the outline of the impression on the finger is deposited in the blood,

02:28:25.630 --> 02:28:27.832
the Amido Black is going to make...

02:28:28.032 --> 02:28:29.267
that impression darker.

02:28:30.768 --> 02:28:34.505
It allows it to have more contrast with the background.

02:28:35.439 --> 02:28:38.476
Bloody fingerprints are very fragile in most cases,

02:28:38.910 --> 02:28:41.946
so they can't be lifted with tape without destroying them,

02:28:42.680 --> 02:28:44.548
even after they're developed with a METO.

02:28:45.750 --> 02:28:46.250
Instead,

02:28:46.584 --> 02:28:48.686
Creighton photographed the enhanced print.

02:28:53.090 --> 02:28:54.425
When he compared it to plants,

02:28:54.926 --> 02:28:55.626
it matched.

02:28:58.262 --> 02:29:03.467
But Plant could have touched the bloodstained doorframe after someone else committed the crime.

02:29:05.069 --> 02:29:10.875
And the evidence against Plant must convince a jury of his guilt beyond a reasonable doubt.

02:29:12.543 --> 02:29:17.515
The irrefutable evidence in the murder of Glenn Michelson had yet to be processed.

02:29:20.418 --> 02:29:23.354
Glenn Michelson had been the victim of a callous murder,

02:29:24.021 --> 02:29:26.157
a ski pole driven through his skull.

02:29:28.526 --> 02:29:30.561
Robert Plant was the prime suspect,

02:29:31.128 --> 02:29:33.698
but could detectives tie him to the crime?

02:29:38.069 --> 02:29:42.506
The answer rested on a bloody palm print left on the grip of the weapon.

02:29:45.343 --> 02:29:46.410
To identify the print,

02:29:46.777 --> 02:29:48.713
John Creighton needed to photograph it.

02:29:49.780 --> 02:29:50.147
First,

02:29:50.214 --> 02:29:51.716
he'd have to make it more visible.

02:29:52.616 --> 02:29:54.752
That was easier said than done.

02:29:57.321 --> 02:30:05.229
The big dilemma was it was a black ski pole grip and it was a dark reddish brown blood impression that was deposited on that.

02:30:06.263 --> 02:30:17.208
Now what I had to do was I had to improve the contrast either by lightening the background of the black ski pole grip or by lightening the blood impression itself.

02:30:18.843 --> 02:30:21.012
Creighton lit the print with a poly light,

02:30:21.579 --> 02:30:24.782
a lamp that can project a wide spectrum of wavelengths.

02:30:25.950 --> 02:30:29.053
The light produced enough contrast to photograph the print.

02:30:30.588 --> 02:30:32.423
But Creighton faced a second problem.

02:30:33.357 --> 02:30:38.496
The curvature of the grip prevented the camera lens from keeping the entire print in focus.

02:30:41.399 --> 02:30:55.446
I had to keep rotating the ski pole grip in order to come up with enough characteristics within the pattern area or within the latent impression that would give me enough information to make an identification.

02:30:57.114 --> 02:30:58.315
By manipulating the grip,

02:30:58.816 --> 02:31:01.552
Creighton was able to get a clear photograph of the print.

02:31:03.220 --> 02:31:04.355
After it was processed,

02:31:04.555 --> 02:31:06.357
he compared the print to Plant's.

02:31:07.258 --> 02:31:07.992
It matched.

02:31:08.859 --> 02:31:12.563
Creighton had placed the murder weapon firmly in Plant's hand.

02:31:15.866 --> 02:31:19.970
The events of Glenn Michelson's final hours now made sense.

02:31:21.105 --> 02:31:22.406
Detectives believed that after the murder,

02:31:22.406 --> 02:31:24.442
After being kicked off Michelson's property,

02:31:24.975 --> 02:31:30.014
Plant hid in the darkness and waited for an opportunity to sneak back into the house.

02:31:31.449 --> 02:31:32.783
Once the guests had left,

02:31:33.017 --> 02:31:35.519
he saw his chance and made his move.

02:31:41.625 --> 02:31:45.596
He slipped into the kitchen and rummaged around until he found a knife.

02:31:54.872 --> 02:31:56.240
As he stepped into the hallway,

02:31:56.674 --> 02:31:57.875
Michelson spotted him.

02:32:00.311 --> 02:32:01.545
The two men struggled,

02:32:02.046 --> 02:32:04.248
but Plant had the fatal advantage.

02:32:05.149 --> 02:32:09.053
He stabbed Michelson repeatedly until he brought the victim down.

02:32:13.624 --> 02:32:16.961
He removed Michelson's boots and put them on his own feet.

02:32:18.028 --> 02:32:21.065
Then he realized his victim wasn't dead.

02:32:26.570 --> 02:32:31.041
So he found a ski pole in another room and returned to finish him off.

02:32:34.011 --> 02:32:35.379
As he thrust the pole,

02:32:35.579 --> 02:32:38.149
he put his hand on the doorframe for support.

02:32:41.685 --> 02:32:42.987
After the final blow,

02:32:43.387 --> 02:32:44.421
he left the house,

02:32:45.022 --> 02:32:47.525
stealing Michelson's car for his getaway.

02:32:48.626 --> 02:32:52.096
But he only made it about a mile before he ran off the road.

02:32:57.501 --> 02:33:05.609
The bloody prints that Creighton analyzed gave Detective Miles Heffernan the evidence he needed to convict Robert Plant.

02:33:09.046 --> 02:33:10.014
It was very compelling,

02:33:10.748 --> 02:33:12.316
very compelling for a jury when they,

02:33:12.850 --> 02:33:14.852
hard to explain or explain away.

02:33:15.352 --> 02:33:19.990
You've got your thumbprint in the victim's blood on the door molding,

02:33:20.457 --> 02:33:23.227
and you've got the handprint on the murder weapon.

02:33:23.761 --> 02:33:25.229
It tells a story right there.

02:33:26.397 --> 02:33:28.098
For the murder of Glenn Michelson,

02:33:28.599 --> 02:33:32.036
Robert Plant received a sentence of 50 years to life.

02:33:34.672 --> 02:33:35.739
For more than a century,

02:33:36.307 --> 02:33:42.346
fingerprints have proven themselves a reliable and irrefutable way to link criminals to their crimes.

02:33:47.985 --> 02:33:48.919
In the next century,

02:33:49.320 --> 02:33:53.524
their role will increase as scientists improve ways to recover them.

02:33:58.095 --> 02:33:58.963
More and more,

02:33:59.530 --> 02:34:04.602
killers will be delivered into the arms of justice by their own hands.

